some of the worst trench

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:47.580
and chemical warfare since World War I,

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:50.100
leaving both countries devastated.

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:52.620
This is the war that
fractured the Middle East

00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:54.780
into lines that still play out

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:57.180
in nearly every conflict there today.

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:59.310
It set the U.S. and Iraq on a path

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:01.980
that ultimately led to
the American invasion.

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:05.010
And it defined Iran's
often hostile relationship

00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:06.210
with the wider world.

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:08.643
This is the Iran-Iraq War.

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:15.727
It's 1979, and Iran is in
the middle of a revolution.

00:23:15.727 --> 00:23:18.090
(crowd chanting)

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:20.550
The country is coming off
of decades of being ruled

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:23.640
by the Shah, a king that the U.S. and UK

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:26.850
had installed in the 50s
after covertly overthrowing

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:29.520
Iran's democratically-elected leader.

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:33.000
But after years of The Shah's
corrupt autocratic rule,

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.880
a mass uprising violently overthrows him,

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:39.150
creating what becomes an Islamic republic,

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:42.114
headed by an Ayatollah
named Ruhollah Khomeini.

00:23:42.114 --> 00:23:43.890
(soft dramatic music)

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:47.910
Khomeini, and a group of
Shia religious scholars,

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:51.690
want to impose fundamentalist
rule on the country

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:54.390
and are hostile to both the Western powers

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:57.090
and the neighboring Soviet Union.

00:23:57.090 --> 00:23:59.040
They establish a military force

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:01.410
called the Islamic Revolutionary Guard,

00:24:01.410 --> 00:24:04.290
which is meant to protect
their hold on the revolution

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:05.910
and to suppress the other groups

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:08.340
who helped overthrow the Shah.

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:10.770
The U.S. is watching all of this in shock.

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:14.340
Their biggest ally in the
region, and a major oil producer,

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:17.283
has just turned from
friend to fierce enemy.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:20.070
But the biggest tremors of this revolution

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:22.134
are felt in the rest of the Middle East.

00:24:22.134 --> 00:24:24.120
(soft tense music)

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:27.510
Iran sends out radio
broadcasts to Arab countries

00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:30.660
like Saudi Arabia,
Lebanon, Kuwait, and Iraq,

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:32.160
calling for people to rise up

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:34.260
and overthrow their own rulers.

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:37.350
They're trying to export their
revolution to their neighbors

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:39.870
to gain new allies in the region.

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:42.960
Neighboring leaders, most
of them Sunni Muslim,

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:44.010
don't like this.

00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:46.530
Saudi Arabia's king fears being overthrown

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:47.910
just like the Shah was.

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:50.280
And in Iraq, Saddam Hussein sees

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:53.430
this Shia religious revolution
on his eastern border

00:24:53.430 --> 00:24:56.550
and worries that the Shia
majority that he rules over

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:58.590
will rise up against him too.

00:24:58.590 --> 00:25:00.693
But he also sees this as an opportunity.

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:03.390
Iran and Iraq have been fighting

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:06.210
over this border territory for years.

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:09.750
This waterway is a critical
access point to the Persian Gulf

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:11.810
where both countries export their oil

00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:13.650
to the rest of the world.

00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:17.160
In a treaty between the two back in 1975,

00:25:17.160 --> 00:25:20.310
Saddam had to give up full
control of this waterway,

00:25:20.310 --> 00:25:25.140
as well as access to oil-rich
regions in what was now Iran.

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.030
Saddam hates this treaty

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:30.570
and is considering taking
the waterway back by force.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:31.830
The Iranian Revolution

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.140
has left the country weak and fractured.

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:36.180
Khomeini and his revolutionary guard

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:39.240
are still fighting other
political factions for control.

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:42.480
The Iranian military,
which used to be funded

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:45.480
and trained by the United
States, is now weak,

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:47.310
its leaders having been jailed

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:50.310
or executed after the revolution.

00:25:50.310 --> 00:25:53.730
Iran's military would
normally be able to crush Iraq

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:56.580
in an invasion, but maybe not anymore.

00:25:56.580 --> 00:26:00.513
Saddam sees this as an opportunity
to weaken a major rival.

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:03.810
Saddam is also riding on a delusion

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:04.950
that he himself is going

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:07.740
to unify the Arab world
under his leadership.

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:09.330
If he invades Iran,

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:12.090
maybe the rest of the Middle
East will rally behind him

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:14.040
against this new common enemy.

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:16.260
He thinks it'll be a quick victory.

00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:18.420
So in September of 1980,

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:21.120
he decides to prepare 10,000 Iraqi troops

00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:23.070
to cross the border to invade Iran.

00:26:23.070 --> 00:26:24.240
(soft dramatic music)
(engine rumbling)

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:25.860
What's about to happen here

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:28.261
isn't just the start of a new war.

00:26:28.261 --> 00:26:30.780
(guns boom)

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:32.610
Saddam is kicking off a dynamic

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:35.883
that will dominate the Middle
East for more than 40 years,

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:42.873
the fight between revolutionary
change and status quo power.

00:26:43.896 --> 00:26:46.563
(subdued music)

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:51.630
The war officially starts

00:26:51.630 --> 00:26:54.210
when Saddam sends his
air force over the border

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:56.910
in a surprise attack on Iranian air bases.

00:26:56.910 --> 00:27:01.200
His 10,000 soldiers cross
from Basra into Southern Iran.

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:03.570
Further north, Saddam opens a second front

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:06.060
to seize strategically-located
border towns

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:08.040
and put pressure on his enemy.

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:10.170
These air attacks mostly fail,

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:12.780
and Iran responds with
airstrikes of their own

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:15.840
into Iraqi territory
hitting oil facilities.

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:18.030
Iran still has sophisticated jets

00:27:18.030 --> 00:27:20.970
that the United States had
given the previous regime.

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:22.720
This gives them an edge in the air.

00:27:23.624 --> 00:27:26.100
(bombs booming)

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:28.770
The war quickly sucks in
the rest of the region.

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:30.660
Israel is among the first.

00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:33.300
It wants to keep these
two adversaries occupied,

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:35.970
fighting with each other,
keeping both sides weak.

00:27:35.970 --> 00:27:37.320
So almost immediately,

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:40.830
Israel secretly sells
supplies and parts to Iran.

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:45.000
This helps the Iranian Air
Force keep planes in the air.

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:47.640
Now, Iran and Israel are not friends,

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:50.850
but Israel sees Iraq as
a greater threat here.

00:27:50.850 --> 00:27:52.350
So they're willing to support.

00:27:53.460 --> 00:27:56.010
Iran needs to get its military organized

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:57.420
if they're gonna fight this war.

00:27:57.420 --> 00:27:59.340
So they release military officers

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:01.440
that they had jailed
during the revolution.

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:04.410
War tends to unify a nation,

00:28:04.410 --> 00:28:07.320
and in this case, the
fractured country of Iran

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:09.960
is unifying around Iraq's invasion,

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:13.110
creating this irony that Saddam is the one

00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:16.113
who actually cements
Khomeini's hold on power.

00:28:17.910 --> 00:28:20.490
Saudi Arabia and Kuwait also step in here,

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:23.820
giving billions of dollars
to fund Iraq's war efforts.

00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:27.000
They want to weaken Iran and
turn back its revolution.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:28.770
The Middle East is now dividing

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:31.920
between, on one side,
Arab states who are ruled

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:35.790
by aging, Sunni monarchies
and strongmen who fear change,

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:40.140
and on the other side, a
Shia Iran bent on revolution,

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:42.453
on remaking the region in its image.

00:28:45.060 --> 00:28:47.490
China is also watching this war.

00:28:47.490 --> 00:28:49.800
They say they will
maintain strict neutrality,

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:51.930
but they take the
opportunity to sell weapons

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:54.450
to both sides of the conflict.

00:28:54.450 --> 00:28:57.397
They eventually become Iran's
top supplier of weapons.

00:28:57.397 --> 00:28:59.280
(subdued music)

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:02.940
For most of 1981, the two
armies are in a stalemate,

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:04.470
with Iraq holding small

00:29:04.470 --> 00:29:07.530
but important stretches
of border territory.

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:09.750
The global powers see this stalemate

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:12.180
and try to negotiate a ceasefire,

00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:13.530
hoping that this conflict

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:16.080
that threatens so much
of the global oil supply

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:17.790
will end soon.

00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:18.810
What they don't know

00:29:18.810 --> 00:29:21.123
is that this is really just the beginning.

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:25.680
Saddam agrees to the ceasefire,
but Khomeini does not.

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:28.860
This war has become so
useful to his goals,

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:31.980
unifying the country and
keeping the military occupied

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:34.170
so that it doesn't become
a rival to his power.

00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:36.810
Khomeini needs the war to continue.

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:40.410
So Iran demands that
Saddam Hussein step down.

00:29:40.410 --> 00:29:43.830
They say the war will continue
until his rule is ended.

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:45.780
Saddam, of course, refuses.

00:29:45.780 --> 00:29:48.300
So Iran goes on the offensive.

00:29:48.300 --> 00:29:50.040
Their forces are now organized.

00:29:50.040 --> 00:29:51.990
Their officers have been freed from jail,

00:29:51.990 --> 00:29:53.850
and they're able to
recapture the territory

00:29:53.850 --> 00:29:57.750
that Saddam had taken,
even pushing into Iraq.

00:29:57.750 --> 00:29:59.940
Saddam's hopes of a quick victory

00:29:59.940 --> 00:30:03.240
and a gain of some valuable
territory are now dead.

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:06.060
So he turns to the
United States for support

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:07.830
and the U.S. agrees to help.

00:30:07.830 --> 00:30:09.660
They hope to weaken Iran

00:30:09.660 --> 00:30:12.930
and its call for anti-American
Islamic revolution

00:30:12.930 --> 00:30:14.070
across the region.

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:16.560
So the U.S. starts giving
Saddam intelligence

00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:19.293
and satellite imagery on
Iranian troop movement.

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:24.630
Iraq also gets another big
backer, the Soviet Union.

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:27.570
The Soviets are at war in
neighboring Afghanistan

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:30.780
trying to suppress another
religious uprising,

00:30:30.780 --> 00:30:32.730
but they also have allies in this region.

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:34.050
So over the course of the war,

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:37.863
the Soviets become a top
supplier of weapons to Iraq.

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:42.450
Now remember the situation
between these two.

00:30:42.450 --> 00:30:44.367
It's a tense moment in the Cold War,

00:30:44.367 --> 00:30:46.260
and the United States and Soviet Union

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:48.330
are suddenly supporting the same side

00:30:48.330 --> 00:30:51.090
of this Middle East conflict,
both backing the country

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:54.330
that will support the status
quo, nothing changing,

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:58.500
allowing their great power
struggle to continue as it has.

00:30:58.500 --> 00:31:00.480
But by the summer of 1982,

00:31:00.480 --> 00:31:03.540
Iran is now pushing into Iraqi territory,

00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:06.480
much of their campaign
focused on the city of Basra.

00:31:06.480 --> 00:31:08.400
If Iran can take Basra,

00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:11.040
it will cut off Iraq
from the Persian Gulf,

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:13.620
preventing them from
shipping out their oil.

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:17.190
Iraqi forces are outnumbered
here, so they start to dig in

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:20.160
and build trenches, erecting
barbed wire barriers,

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:22.710
planting landmines, and
surrounding the city

00:31:22.710 --> 00:31:24.990
to stop Iran from taking it.

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:26.340
The fighting starts to resemble

00:31:26.340 --> 00:31:28.530
the brutal warfare of World War I,

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:30.570
waves of artillery strikes,

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:33.960
and the Iranian military
resorting to human wave attacks

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:37.410
where thousands of boys and
men charge over open fields

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:40.560
leading to huge numbers of casualties.

00:31:40.560 --> 00:31:43.110
Iran is not successful in taking the city,

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:45.690
but this attack does weaken Saddam,

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:47.140
showing that he's vulnerable.

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:50.400
And this is where we
need to look to the north

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:51.789
to talk about the Kurds.

00:31:51.789 --> 00:31:53.340
(subdued music)

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:55.980
The Kurds are a minority
group in the north of Iraq.

00:31:55.980 --> 00:31:58.470
They've been long suppressed
by Saddam Hussein,

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:00.600
and they see this moment as an opportunity

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:03.600
to break away from Iraq
to make their own country.

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:06.570
So Kurdish forces start
fighting with the Iraqi army,

00:32:06.570 --> 00:32:08.610
taking towns and villages.

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:11.130
So now Saddam is fighting
two different armies,

00:32:11.130 --> 00:32:13.143
one of which lives in his own country.

00:32:14.760 --> 00:32:17.580
Iran starts sending support to the Kurds

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:19.770
who have a presence in this mountainous,

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:21.630
oil-rich part of Iraq.

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:23.040
If the Kurds can hold it

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:26.280
and keep Saddam's regime
away from all of this oil,

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:29.070
it would have a major effect on this war.

00:32:29.070 --> 00:32:32.340
This is a big deal for Saddam,
and he switches tactics

00:32:32.340 --> 00:32:35.640
to make sure he can
control this northern area.

00:32:35.640 --> 00:32:38.940
He escalates by using chemical weapons

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:42.300
against the Kurds as well as the Iranians.

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:44.700
Shells filled with mustard gas,

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:48.120
weapons that cause extreme
burning and blindness,

00:32:48.120 --> 00:32:50.850
a weapon that is illegal
under international law,

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:52.680
but even still, the global powers

00:32:52.680 --> 00:32:55.800
who are supporting him
mostly look the other way.

00:32:55.800 --> 00:32:59.250
The U.S. starts sending
technology and money to Saddam,

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:02.730
even restoring official
diplomatic ties with Iraq.

00:33:02.730 --> 00:33:06.090
This allows Iraq to buy
technology from the United States

00:33:06.090 --> 00:33:09.540
that helps them develop
their weapons programs,

00:33:09.540 --> 00:33:13.410
including the horrific
chemical and biological weapons

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:16.020
that he will soon be
using on the battlefield.

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:19.920
They need him to keep fighting
because this war has changed.

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:22.920
It went from protecting
Saddam from revolution

00:33:22.920 --> 00:33:26.160
to now using Saddam to weaken Iran.

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:27.870
So they keep supporting him,

00:33:27.870 --> 00:33:30.120
showing him that he can
use chemical weapons

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:31.800
without being punished.

00:33:31.800 --> 00:33:35.400
The chemical attacks help
push back Iranian forces,

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:37.683
and the war reaches a new stalemate.

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:46.083
1984 is the year that the oil
war begins in this conflict.

00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:49.650
Iraq starts attacking Iranian oil tankers

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:53.100
in the Persian Gulf using
new jets provided by France,

00:33:53.100 --> 00:33:55.470
a new entrant to this conflict.

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:58.230
Saddam warns that he'll
also attack any ship

00:33:58.230 --> 00:34:00.270
going into Iranian ports.

00:34:00.270 --> 00:34:02.880
Iran retaliates by attacking oil tankers

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:04.320
carrying Iraq's oil.

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:06.990
The oil that the entire globe relies on

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:10.380
is now at the center of this
increasingly brutal conflict.

00:34:10.380 --> 00:34:14.190
Hundreds of commercial ships
are attacked by both sides,

00:34:14.190 --> 00:34:17.670
resulting in the death of
over 400 civilian sailors.

00:34:17.670 --> 00:34:19.530
The U.S. has to send in two frigates

00:34:19.530 --> 00:34:22.020
and a guided missile destroyer
into the Persian Gulf

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:23.880
to escort U.S. ships,

00:34:23.880 --> 00:34:26.493
hoping that their presence
will stop these attacks.

00:34:28.230 --> 00:34:30.990
Meanwhile, Iran is making
progress on the ground,

00:34:30.990 --> 00:34:33.270
slowly taking territory from Iraq

00:34:33.270 --> 00:34:36.359
and continuing attacks on
the vital port city of Basra.

00:34:36.359 --> 00:34:37.620
(soft grim music)

00:34:37.620 --> 00:34:42.063
It's 1985 and Saddam is about
to escalate to a new extreme.

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:46.200
He starts shooting
missiles and dropping bombs

00:34:46.200 --> 00:34:48.750
on Iranian cities all over the country,

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:49.920
including the capitol.

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:54.060
This kills 16,000 people
and leaves many homeless.

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:57.450
Iran responds, striking Iraqi cities,

00:34:57.450 --> 00:34:59.490
firing these massive missiles,

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:01.743
primarily at the capital of Baghdad.

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:06.750
These strikes hit civilian targets

00:35:06.750 --> 00:35:10.200
like a school and a bus station
with hundreds of casualties.

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:12.930
Civilians on both sides are
now caught in the crossfire

00:35:12.930 --> 00:35:15.060
of this brutally escalating war.

00:35:15.060 --> 00:35:18.690
Their cities and their homes
are not safe, so many flee.

00:35:18.690 --> 00:35:21.360
These events leave an
entire generation scarred

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:25.020
by the trauma of war and a
disdain for the outside powers

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:26.870
that have ripped their country apart.

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:30.960
And speaking of outside powers,

00:35:30.960 --> 00:35:33.240
this is when the U.S.
starts double dealing.

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:35.580
They start selling missiles to Iran,

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:39.510
which gives Iran an edge
against Iraqi forces

00:35:39.510 --> 00:35:42.540
who the U.S. is also supporting.

00:35:42.540 --> 00:35:44.910
Behind all this is
President Ronald Reagan,

00:35:44.910 --> 00:35:47.940
who wants to use the money from this deal

00:35:47.940 --> 00:35:51.183
to fund an anti-communist
militia group in Nicaragua.

00:35:52.260 --> 00:35:54.000
It's all supposed to be a secret,

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:55.500
but will eventually come out

00:35:55.500 --> 00:35:58.083
and be known as the Iran-Contra scandal.

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:02.490
Meanwhile, Kuwait is asking
the U.S. to be more aggressive

00:36:02.490 --> 00:36:04.350
to protect their oil tankers.

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:07.470
Iranian attacks on their ships
have started to take a toll.

00:36:07.470 --> 00:36:10.560
The U.S. is getting more
involved in the war.

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:13.320
Their presence in the
Persian Gulf is growing.

00:36:13.320 --> 00:36:17.190
They start putting American
flags on Kuwaiti tankers

00:36:17.190 --> 00:36:19.143
to deter Iran from attacking them.

00:36:21.433 --> 00:36:23.400
And then in March of 1988,

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.710
Iran partners with the Kurdish fighters

00:36:25.710 --> 00:36:28.170
to take over the Iraqi town of Halabja.

00:36:28.170 --> 00:36:31.170
The Kurds know that this
is going to set Saddam off,

00:36:31.170 --> 00:36:34.050
and they brace themselves
for a brutal retaliation.

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:35.250
But what comes next

00:36:35.250 --> 00:36:38.370
is worse than anyone
could have prepared for.

00:36:38.370 --> 00:36:40.440
The Iraqi army drops bombs

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:43.440
and artillery shells
containing deadly chemicals,

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:47.010
blanketing the entire town
with a cloud of deadly gas

00:36:47.010 --> 00:36:48.540
that sinks into homes

00:36:48.540 --> 00:36:50.700
and buildings and the underground shelters

00:36:50.700 --> 00:36:53.400
where many were hiding for protection.

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:57.810
It's a brutal attack that
kills over 5,000 Iraqi Kurds,

00:36:57.810 --> 00:36:59.763
mostly unarmed civilians.

00:37:00.690 --> 00:37:03.330
And it was a part of a broader campaign

00:37:03.330 --> 00:37:04.980
that Saddam was now waging

00:37:04.980 --> 00:37:07.710
to wipe out the Kurdish people entirely.

00:37:07.710 --> 00:37:10.700
He now had what he believed
was a justification.

00:37:10.700 --> 00:37:14.310
So the Iraqi Army starts
going village to village,

00:37:14.310 --> 00:37:16.650
dropping chemical weapons on civilians

00:37:16.650 --> 00:37:18.633
and executing any survivors.

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:23.820
This resulted in an estimated
50 to 100,000 deaths

00:37:23.820 --> 00:37:26.340
in what has now been deemed as a genocide,

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:29.283
one of the many war crimes
committed by Saddam Hussein.

00:37:30.900 --> 00:37:33.300
The U.S. knows this is happening,

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:36.090
and yet, according to
now-public documents,

00:37:36.090 --> 00:37:39.360
the official government line
was to turn a blind eye,

00:37:39.360 --> 00:37:41.133
to blame everything on Iran.

00:37:43.650 --> 00:37:45.660
Saddam's horrific war crimes

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:48.810
project a new fear onto
the civilians in Iran

00:37:48.810 --> 00:37:51.360
who now worry that a missile
loaded with deadly gas

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:53.160
could land on their cities.

00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:55.560
Many flee to take
shelter in the mountains.

00:37:55.560 --> 00:37:59.400
And by now, after eight
years of devastating war,

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:02.340
Iran's economy and social order is frayed.

00:38:02.340 --> 00:38:04.140
Their leader, Ayatollah Khomeini,

00:38:04.140 --> 00:38:06.543
is feeling the pressure to end this war.

00:38:09.120 --> 00:38:12.330
More than a million lives
have been lost in this war.

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:15.300
All sides are looking for a way out.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:17.970
And the UN Security Council
is pressuring the parties

00:38:17.970 --> 00:38:19.890
to accept a ceasefire.

00:38:19.890 --> 00:38:24.420
Then in July of 1988, a U.S.
ship in the Persian Gulf

00:38:24.420 --> 00:38:26.750
shoots down an Iranian passenger jet,

00:38:26.750 --> 00:38:30.000
(soft tense music)

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:33.180
killing all 290 civilians on board.

00:38:33.180 --> 00:38:35.820
The U.S. shakes it off as
an unfortunate accident,

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:37.860
saying that they mistook the airliner

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:40.230
for a hostile F-16 fighter jet.

00:38:40.230 --> 00:38:42.600
They pay compensation to
the victim's families,

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:45.360
and President Reagan sends
a letter apologizing,

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:48.150
but the government never
formally apologizes.

00:38:48.150 --> 00:38:50.070
And the captain of the ship who did this

00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:53.640
later received a medal which
fed a deep suspicion in Iran

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:55.410
that this attack was deliberate,

00:38:55.410 --> 00:38:59.880
that it was a scheme to coerce
them into accepting peace.

00:38:59.880 --> 00:39:01.800
Fear of more American attacks,

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:03.870
along with a new offensive by Iraq,

00:39:03.870 --> 00:39:06.930
pushes the Iranian forces to
withdraw from the country,

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:09.627
and both sides finally accept a ceasefire.

00:39:09.627 --> 00:39:12.780
(subdued music)

00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:16.230
The war is finally over,
and after all of this,

00:39:16.230 --> 00:39:19.800
the borders were unchanged
from before the fighting.

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:23.310
But in some ways, this was
really just the first round

00:39:23.310 --> 00:39:26.730
in a larger conflict that has
continued through to today,

00:39:26.730 --> 00:39:29.430
a war for the future of the Middle East,

00:39:29.430 --> 00:39:32.310
one that the United States
is deeply involved in

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:35.250
but that most expresses
itself as a cold war

00:39:35.250 --> 00:39:38.100
between Saudi Arabia and Iran,

00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:40.860
who now fuel civil wars around the region

00:39:40.860 --> 00:39:42.930
in Lebanon, Syria, and Yemen,

00:39:42.930 --> 00:39:44.880
backing their own proxy fighters

00:39:44.880 --> 00:39:47.010
and making these conflicts worse.

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:49.500
But another major takeaway
here is that this war

00:39:49.500 --> 00:39:52.980
defined Iran's relationship
to the rest of the world,

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:54.840
especially the United States.

00:39:54.840 --> 00:39:58.980
Iranian hardliners came to
feel confirmed in their belief

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:01.110
that they could never
trust an outside world

00:40:01.110 --> 00:40:03.210
bent on Iran's destruction

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:05.910
and seemingly unconstrained
in their violence,

00:40:05.910 --> 00:40:08.850
leading some leaders to conclude
that only nuclear weapons

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:11.970
could stave off disaster from the outside.

00:40:11.970 --> 00:40:15.360
Saddam Hussein's Iraq
emerged battered by this war,

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:18.030
humiliated by this failure against Iran.

00:40:18.030 --> 00:40:21.330
He faced growing internal
resistance from his people,

00:40:21.330 --> 00:40:24.450
which he suppressed with
more and more violence.

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:27.210
He also came out of
this with huge war debts

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:29.340
to Kuwait and Saudi Arabia.

00:40:29.340 --> 00:40:30.570
Angry that his allies

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:33.060
had billed him to fight
a war on their behalf,

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:37.140
a few years later, Saddam
invades Kuwait seeking its oil,

00:40:37.140 --> 00:40:39.930
taking the spoils that
he felt he was owed.

00:40:39.930 --> 00:40:42.780
And this is when his one-time
backer, the United States,

00:40:42.780 --> 00:40:46.110
quickly turns against him,
expelling him from Kuwait

00:40:46.110 --> 00:40:49.290
and beating him back in
a swift 43-day victory,

00:40:49.290 --> 00:40:51.360
cutting him off from the world economy

00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:54.030
and turning him into a pariah, an enemy.

00:40:54.030 --> 00:40:56.850
A decade after Saddam had
gassed his own people,

00:40:56.850 --> 00:40:58.890
the U.S. finally blamed him for it,

00:40:58.890 --> 00:41:01.710
turning it into a byword for his cruelty

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:04.770
and the centerpiece for their
demand that he step down.

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:07.080
A few years later, George W. Bush

00:41:07.080 --> 00:41:10.110
would cite these weapons
of mass destructions,

00:41:10.110 --> 00:41:11.730
the ones that he would never find,

00:41:11.730 --> 00:41:15.660
as a reason to invade the
country to topple Saddam.

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:19.080
Bush's war room was filled
with the same officials

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:20.520
who had covered for Saddam

00:41:20.520 --> 00:41:22.740
and his war crimes in the first place,

00:41:22.740 --> 00:41:24.450
and making it so crystal clear

00:41:24.450 --> 00:41:25.680
that, even though this war

00:41:25.680 --> 00:41:28.710
between Iran and Iraq ended decades ago,

00:41:28.710 --> 00:41:32.340
the Middle East we see today
is built off of what happened

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:34.380
during those eight brutal years.

00:41:34.380 --> 00:41:36.930
Before we go into the next
video, I just want to remind you

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:40.230
that this is all made possible
because of Ground News,

00:41:40.230 --> 00:41:41.940
the sponsor of this compilation.

00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:44.220
Ground News helps me do these deep dives

00:41:44.220 --> 00:41:47.010
by helping me compare lots of headlines

00:41:47.010 --> 00:41:49.020
and revealing the different biases

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:51.960
and reliability of the
facts in those headlines.

