01:48:38.333 --> 01:48:39.180
(subdued music)

01:48:39.180 --> 01:48:42.090
Along the banks of the most
important waterway on earth

01:48:42.090 --> 01:48:44.160
sits the country of Yemen,

01:48:44.160 --> 01:48:46.800
a place that was two countries for decades

01:48:46.800 --> 01:48:48.510
until the year 1990,

01:48:48.510 --> 01:48:50.190
when it was unified into one.

01:48:50.190 --> 01:48:52.080
A fragile patchwork of groups

01:48:52.080 --> 01:48:54.990
all with different tribal
backgrounds, religious beliefs,

01:48:54.990 --> 01:48:56.970
and political aspirations.

01:48:56.970 --> 01:48:59.760
The story I want to
tell starts in the '90s,

01:48:59.760 --> 01:49:03.030
when one group forms up in the
northern mountains of Yemen.

01:49:03.030 --> 01:49:05.790
It's started by a group
of religious Yemenis

01:49:05.790 --> 01:49:08.190
who call themselves the Believing Youth,

01:49:08.190 --> 01:49:12.180
who were members of a Shiite
Muslim sect called the Zaidi.

01:49:12.180 --> 01:49:14.190
Zaidi Muslims used to be the majority

01:49:14.190 --> 01:49:16.080
when the country was split in two.

01:49:16.080 --> 01:49:18.030
But in the new unified Yemen,

01:49:18.030 --> 01:49:19.560
they became a minority

01:49:19.560 --> 01:49:22.410
and felt neglected and
discriminated against.

01:49:22.410 --> 01:49:24.000
At first, the group focuses

01:49:24.000 --> 01:49:26.910
on rebuilding the Zaidi
religious identity,

01:49:26.910 --> 01:49:28.710
hosting summer camps for young men

01:49:28.710 --> 01:49:30.900
and offering religious education.

01:49:30.900 --> 01:49:32.700
But in the late 1990s,

01:49:32.700 --> 01:49:35.223
the group starts to
change in important ways.

01:49:36.480 --> 01:49:39.630
A member of Yemen's
parliament resigns in protest

01:49:39.630 --> 01:49:41.253
and joins the Believing Youth.

01:49:42.240 --> 01:49:44.310
He felt that the government
of Yemen was corrupt

01:49:44.310 --> 01:49:45.720
and far too influenced

01:49:45.720 --> 01:49:48.543
by Yemen's neighbor to
the north, Saudi Arabia.

01:49:51.150 --> 01:49:53.760
Al Houthi blamed outside influence

01:49:53.760 --> 01:49:55.650
for many of the country's problems.

01:49:55.650 --> 01:49:57.240
So as he leaves the government,

01:49:57.240 --> 01:49:58.680
Al Houthi uses his wealth

01:49:58.680 --> 01:50:01.170
and family connections
in the Zaidi community

01:50:01.170 --> 01:50:03.540
to become the leader
of the Believing Youth.

01:50:03.540 --> 01:50:05.760
He wants an Islamic revolution,

01:50:05.760 --> 01:50:07.710
modeled after the one in Iran.

01:50:07.710 --> 01:50:09.780
One that kicks out any foreign influence

01:50:09.780 --> 01:50:11.266
and restores Islamic values.

01:50:11.266 --> 01:50:13.503
- [Reporter] Outrage with
the speed of the human shout.

01:50:13.503 --> 01:50:15.390
- [Johnny] With Al Houthi's influence,

01:50:15.390 --> 01:50:18.120
this group of young men in the
mountainous region of Yemen

01:50:18.120 --> 01:50:19.800
starts to change.

01:50:19.800 --> 01:50:23.520
They begin protesting Yemen's
president, Ali Abdullah Saleh.

01:50:23.520 --> 01:50:27.630
Soon they start taking up
weapons, taking over territory,

01:50:27.630 --> 01:50:30.060
and eventually setting up
their own security checkpoints

01:50:30.060 --> 01:50:31.350
in their region.

01:50:31.350 --> 01:50:32.700
The Believing Youth are starting

01:50:32.700 --> 01:50:34.530
to look a lot less like a summer camp

01:50:34.530 --> 01:50:37.560
and a lot more like a
militia, like a rebellion.

01:50:37.560 --> 01:50:38.580
President Saleh,

01:50:38.580 --> 01:50:40.770
who had previously supported
the Believing Youth,

01:50:40.770 --> 01:50:42.300
is starting to get worried.

01:50:42.300 --> 01:50:44.100
And then in 2001,

01:50:44.100 --> 01:50:46.043
the entire Middle East
would change forever.

01:50:46.043 --> 01:50:46.876
(dark music)

01:50:46.876 --> 01:50:49.928
After 9/11, the U.S.
launches a war on terror.

01:50:49.928 --> 01:50:53.190
(reflective music)

01:50:53.190 --> 01:50:55.170
They asked President Saleh for help,

01:50:55.170 --> 01:50:57.870
and he cooperates, taking military aid

01:50:57.870 --> 01:50:59.640
and allowing American special forces

01:50:59.640 --> 01:51:01.320
to operate inside Yemen,

01:51:01.320 --> 01:51:04.023
where the terrorist group
Al-Qaeda has a presence.

01:51:05.010 --> 01:51:06.270
18 months later,

01:51:06.270 --> 01:51:09.000
the U.S. presence in the
region would balloon even more

01:51:09.000 --> 01:51:13.020
when the American military
invades and occupies Iraq.

01:51:13.020 --> 01:51:15.420
For Al Houthi and his group of young men,

01:51:15.420 --> 01:51:19.530
this crosses a red line and
deeply radicalizes the movement.

01:51:19.530 --> 01:51:21.810
Al Houthi criticizes
the president publicly

01:51:21.810 --> 01:51:25.140
for pandering to what he
sees as American imperialism

01:51:25.140 --> 01:51:26.280
in Muslim lands.

01:51:26.280 --> 01:51:28.140
And many in Yemen agree,

01:51:28.140 --> 01:51:31.680
joining the movement and
bolstering their numbers.

01:51:31.680 --> 01:51:33.840
Al Houthi adopts the violent slogan

01:51:33.840 --> 01:51:36.630
that sits on the flag of
his movement to this day:

01:51:36.630 --> 01:51:39.420
God is great. Death to America.

01:51:39.420 --> 01:51:42.210
Death to Israel. Curse on the Jews.

01:51:42.210 --> 01:51:43.773
Victory to Islam.

01:51:45.060 --> 01:51:47.460
President Saleh feels
threatened by the growth

01:51:47.460 --> 01:51:49.410
of this increasingly radical movement

01:51:49.410 --> 01:51:52.110
that is taking control of
territory in the north.

01:51:52.110 --> 01:51:55.350
He issues an arrest warrant
and a bounty for Al Houthi

01:51:55.350 --> 01:51:57.840
and brutally cracks
down on their protests.

01:51:57.840 --> 01:52:00.870
In response, the Believing
Youth launch an armed rebellion

01:52:00.870 --> 01:52:02.070
in the north of Yemen,

01:52:02.070 --> 01:52:05.040
leading to counterattacks
by the Yemeni military.

01:52:05.040 --> 01:52:06.900
Shortly after the fighting begins,

01:52:06.900 --> 01:52:09.513
Al Houthi is killed in
a cave in the north.

01:52:10.710 --> 01:52:15.120
The rebels he leaves behind
fight on with new conviction,

01:52:15.120 --> 01:52:18.390
and the world would know them
by the name of their martyr,

01:52:18.390 --> 01:52:20.730
a symbol of imperial resistance

01:52:20.730 --> 01:52:23.200
that would soon be
firing rockets at Israel

01:52:24.810 --> 01:52:27.707
and hijacking cargo ships in the Red Sea.

01:52:27.707 --> 01:52:30.374
(subdued music)

01:52:35.201 --> 01:52:37.380
By 2004, the Houthis have morphed

01:52:37.380 --> 01:52:39.210
into a full-blown rebellion,

01:52:39.210 --> 01:52:40.740
fighting against Yemen's government

01:52:40.740 --> 01:52:42.630
from their northern strongholds.

01:52:42.630 --> 01:52:44.160
They know this land well

01:52:44.160 --> 01:52:45.810
and they have local support,

01:52:45.810 --> 01:52:47.790
allowing them to fight
a guerrilla campaign

01:52:47.790 --> 01:52:49.440
against the government forces,

01:52:49.440 --> 01:52:52.650
blending in with civilians,
hiding in mountains and caves,

01:52:52.650 --> 01:52:54.000
and allowing them to resist

01:52:54.000 --> 01:52:56.160
the much better-equipped Yemeni army.

01:52:56.160 --> 01:52:58.170
As the conflict turns more violent,

01:52:58.170 --> 01:53:00.090
more tribes in the region would feel

01:53:00.090 --> 01:53:02.370
that the military is being overly brutal,

01:53:02.370 --> 01:53:04.470
leading them to join the Houthis.

01:53:04.470 --> 01:53:07.620
And it's at this time that the
conflict goes international.

01:53:07.620 --> 01:53:09.540
The Houthis say that Saudi Arabia

01:53:09.540 --> 01:53:10.980
is letting the government of Yemen

01:53:10.980 --> 01:53:13.230
position troops right across this border.

01:53:13.230 --> 01:53:15.390
So they invade into Saudi Arabia,

01:53:15.390 --> 01:53:18.060
killing Saudi soldiers
and taking hostages.

01:53:18.060 --> 01:53:21.060
This pulls Saudi Arabia into the conflict,

01:53:21.060 --> 01:53:23.550
as they respond with airstrikes and troops

01:53:23.550 --> 01:53:25.800
and push the Houthis
out of their territory,

01:53:25.800 --> 01:53:27.480
punishing them for this invasion.

01:53:27.480 --> 01:53:30.300
Now, over here, Iran is watching this war,

01:53:30.300 --> 01:53:31.830
and they see an opportunity.

01:53:31.830 --> 01:53:33.690
The Houthis are now fighting directly

01:53:33.690 --> 01:53:36.630
against their biggest rival, Saudi Arabia.

01:53:36.630 --> 01:53:39.990
So they start secretly sending
weapons to the Houthis.

01:53:39.990 --> 01:53:42.330
At least that's what Saudi Arabia claims,

01:53:42.330 --> 01:53:44.700
saying that they
intercepted an Iranian ship

01:53:44.700 --> 01:53:47.010
filled with weapons and military trainers

01:53:47.010 --> 01:53:48.690
on their way to the Houthis.

01:53:48.690 --> 01:53:52.260
It looks like Iran is directly
supporting the Houthis now.

01:53:52.260 --> 01:53:55.140
Though how much support they
provide behind the scenes

01:53:55.140 --> 01:53:57.960
will always be intentionally unclear.

01:53:57.960 --> 01:53:59.127
As the conflict between the Houthis

01:53:59.127 --> 01:54:01.350
and the government continues in the north,

01:54:01.350 --> 01:54:04.800
Saudi Arabia has been fighting
their own war on terror

01:54:04.800 --> 01:54:08.220
against a branch of Al-Qaeda
operating in Saudi Arabia.

01:54:08.220 --> 01:54:09.870
Seeing that their days are numbered,

01:54:09.870 --> 01:54:12.330
this branch of Al-Qaeda flees south

01:54:12.330 --> 01:54:15.540
over the border into Yemen,
where they merge their forces

01:54:15.540 --> 01:54:17.580
with the Yemen branch of Al-Qaeda.

01:54:17.580 --> 01:54:22.230
They create Al-Qaeda in the
Arabian Peninsula, or AQAP,

01:54:22.230 --> 01:54:24.690
one of the most dangerous
and well-funded versions

01:54:24.690 --> 01:54:26.610
of the group to ever exist.

01:54:26.610 --> 01:54:29.610
The group's goal is to
create a strict Islamic state

01:54:29.610 --> 01:54:31.200
across the whole region,

01:54:31.200 --> 01:54:33.060
while violently waging holy war

01:54:33.060 --> 01:54:34.930
against foreigners in Muslim lands.

01:54:34.930 --> 01:54:37.847
(reflective music)

01:54:38.730 --> 01:54:41.550
This puts them at odds with
both the Yemeni government

01:54:41.550 --> 01:54:43.440
and the Houthis.

01:54:43.440 --> 01:54:45.783
The conflict is getting more complicated.

01:54:46.620 --> 01:54:50.490
Around 2011, the region
starts to change in big ways.

01:54:50.490 --> 01:54:52.350
In one country after another,

01:54:52.350 --> 01:54:55.440
there's an outpouring of
public anger on the streets,

01:54:55.440 --> 01:54:57.540
demanding change from their government,

01:54:57.540 --> 01:54:59.280
removing old leaders.

01:54:59.280 --> 01:55:00.930
This is called the Arab Spring.

01:55:00.930 --> 01:55:04.694
It's upheaval that spirals the
region into hope and chaos.

01:55:04.694 --> 01:55:09.527
(protesters chanting in foreign language)

01:55:15.900 --> 01:55:18.690
- [Johnny] Iran and Saudi Arabia
see this as an opportunity

01:55:18.690 --> 01:55:21.990
to strategically choose sides
in all of these countries,

01:55:21.990 --> 01:55:24.060
in hopes that when the dust settles,

01:55:24.060 --> 01:55:26.400
they will come out with
a stronger hand of allies

01:55:26.400 --> 01:55:27.450
in the region.

01:55:27.450 --> 01:55:30.060
And one of their main focuses is Yemen,

01:55:30.060 --> 01:55:31.890
a country that is also rising up,

01:55:31.890 --> 01:55:34.620
calling for President Saleh to step down.

01:55:34.620 --> 01:55:38.400
The corrupt dictator of
Yemen is running out of time,

01:55:38.400 --> 01:55:39.870
which is something the Houthis

01:55:39.870 --> 01:55:41.703
have been fighting for for years.

01:55:43.530 --> 01:55:47.760
But Saleh refuses to step down,
adding fuel to the uprising.

01:55:47.760 --> 01:55:50.940
Saudi Arabia sees this situation
at their southern border

01:55:50.940 --> 01:55:53.910
and decides to step in
with the help of the UN,

01:55:53.910 --> 01:55:57.300
convincing Saleh to transfer
power to his vice president,

01:55:57.300 --> 01:55:59.790
in exchange for immunity from prosecution.

01:55:59.790 --> 01:56:02.850
On his way out, Saleh takes
the billions of dollars

01:56:02.850 --> 01:56:05.490
that he corruptly amassed
during his time in office,

01:56:05.490 --> 01:56:07.200
and he also retains the loyalty

01:56:07.200 --> 01:56:09.150
of key parts of the military,

01:56:09.150 --> 01:56:11.160
fighters that are only loyal to him.

01:56:11.160 --> 01:56:12.660
But the people aren't satisfied

01:56:12.660 --> 01:56:15.060
with this new Saudi-backed president.

01:56:15.060 --> 01:56:17.670
The government remains
weak and unorganized.

01:56:17.670 --> 01:56:20.430
Meanwhile, the Houthis
are taking advantage

01:56:20.430 --> 01:56:23.640
of this infighting and
only getting stronger,

01:56:23.640 --> 01:56:26.310
taking more and more
territory from the government,

01:56:26.310 --> 01:56:27.720
adding to their forces,

01:56:27.720 --> 01:56:30.750
and receiving more support
from their backer, Iran,

01:56:30.750 --> 01:56:33.540
who is supplying them with
more training, missiles,

01:56:33.540 --> 01:56:35.790
drones, and other advanced weapons.

01:56:35.790 --> 01:56:38.940
They use these weapons in the fall of 2014

01:56:38.940 --> 01:56:40.860
to march into southern Yemen,

01:56:40.860 --> 01:56:43.143
taking over large swaths of territory.

01:56:45.690 --> 01:56:47.370
Now that the Houthis have momentum,

01:56:47.370 --> 01:56:50.100
the former President Saleh joins them

01:56:50.100 --> 01:56:52.080
in an alliance of convenience.

01:56:52.080 --> 01:56:54.750
In fact, Saleh's forces are critical

01:56:54.750 --> 01:56:57.930
in helping the Houthis take
the capital city of Sanaa.

01:56:57.930 --> 01:57:00.750
Saleh is now working
with his former enemy,

01:57:00.750 --> 01:57:02.760
against his own vice president,

01:57:02.760 --> 01:57:05.280
the man he had to give power
to when he stepped down.

01:57:05.280 --> 01:57:07.800
President Hadi and his
government flee south

01:57:07.800 --> 01:57:09.360
as the Houthis take over,

01:57:09.360 --> 01:57:11.260
but the Houthis are right behind them.

01:57:12.420 --> 01:57:14.940
So Hadi has to flee to Saudi Arabia,

01:57:14.940 --> 01:57:16.743
trying to govern Yemen from there.

01:57:17.992 --> 01:57:20.160
But the Houthis just
keep gaining momentum.

01:57:20.160 --> 01:57:21.870
By the middle of 2015,

01:57:21.870 --> 01:57:24.030
they now control the
capital city completely,

01:57:24.030 --> 01:57:25.650
where they create their own government

01:57:25.650 --> 01:57:28.410
and take control of the
government's military weapons,

01:57:28.410 --> 01:57:31.410
helping them expand even
further into the south.

01:57:31.410 --> 01:57:33.210
Now that the Houthis are in power,

01:57:33.210 --> 01:57:36.510
they run an oppressive regime
that silences free speech,

01:57:36.510 --> 01:57:39.270
crushes dissent through
arrest and torture,

01:57:39.270 --> 01:57:41.400
and recruits children as soldiers,

01:57:41.400 --> 01:57:43.710
all while imposing strict religious laws

01:57:43.710 --> 01:57:45.750
that undermine women's rights.

01:57:45.750 --> 01:57:48.990
Meanwhile, Saudi Arabia
is getting nervous.

01:57:48.990 --> 01:57:50.850
Their southern neighbor is now controlled

01:57:50.850 --> 01:57:54.000
by a group that is funded
by their biggest enemy.

01:57:54.000 --> 01:57:56.550
So they recruit the support of countries

01:57:56.550 --> 01:57:59.190
around the region and invade Yemen,

01:57:59.190 --> 01:58:01.980
with the goal of removing
the Houthis from power.

01:58:01.980 --> 01:58:05.130
The war is led by Crown
Prince Mohammed bin Salman,

01:58:05.130 --> 01:58:08.430
Saudi Arabia's up-and-coming
leader and defense minister.

01:58:08.430 --> 01:58:10.860
He starts with a
widespread bombing campaign

01:58:10.860 --> 01:58:12.000
and a naval blockade

01:58:12.000 --> 01:58:14.430
that prevents shipping in
and out of the country,

01:58:14.430 --> 01:58:16.650
with the goal of trying to cut off support

01:58:16.650 --> 01:58:18.030
coming in from Iran.

01:58:18.030 --> 01:58:20.490
The crown prince turns
to the United States

01:58:20.490 --> 01:58:22.830
in need of more weapons for this war.

01:58:22.830 --> 01:58:25.140
While the U.S. leaders are
reluctant to get involved

01:58:25.140 --> 01:58:27.150
in another Middle East conflict,

01:58:27.150 --> 01:58:29.640
they also want to maintain
a good relationship

01:58:29.640 --> 01:58:32.400
with Saudi Arabia, who
is their major security

01:58:32.400 --> 01:58:34.260
and energy partner in the region.

01:58:34.260 --> 01:58:35.370
They also have an interest

01:58:35.370 --> 01:58:38.250
in weakening Al-Qaeda's
growing presence in Yemen.

01:58:38.250 --> 01:58:40.140
So in addition to supporting this war

01:58:40.140 --> 01:58:42.030
with logistics and intelligence,

01:58:42.030 --> 01:58:45.510
President Obama approves
billions of dollars of weapons:

01:58:45.510 --> 01:58:49.440
planes, tanks, and even weapons
banned by most of the world

01:58:49.440 --> 01:58:51.270
for how dangerous they are for civilians,

01:58:51.270 --> 01:58:52.622
like cluster munitions.

01:58:52.622 --> 01:58:55.122
(tense music)

01:58:57.330 --> 01:59:00.570
This quickly turns Saudi Arabia
into the largest importer

01:59:00.570 --> 01:59:03.300
of American weapons for
the next four years.

01:59:03.300 --> 01:59:04.860
So it's 2015,

01:59:04.860 --> 01:59:07.800
and Yemen is in a full-blown
war between the Houthis

01:59:07.800 --> 01:59:09.900
and Saudi Arabia and their coalition.

01:59:09.900 --> 01:59:12.420
This is when Al-Qaeda,
who's not on either side,

01:59:12.420 --> 01:59:14.040
takes advantage of this chaos

01:59:14.040 --> 01:59:16.110
and makes a land grab
in the middle of Yemen.

01:59:16.110 --> 01:59:19.110
They take major cities and
clash with government forces.

01:59:19.110 --> 01:59:21.240
Also taking advantage of the instability

01:59:21.240 --> 01:59:23.520
is the terrorist group ISIS,

01:59:23.520 --> 01:59:27.360
who come to Yemen and fight
the Houthis and Al-Qaeda,

01:59:27.360 --> 01:59:28.710
at the same time.

01:59:28.710 --> 01:59:30.450
Though their attacks are scattered,

01:59:30.450 --> 01:59:32.580
and they don't control
significant territory.

01:59:32.580 --> 01:59:35.790
The U.S. then gets more
involved by launching airstrikes

01:59:35.790 --> 01:59:37.920
at Al-Qaeda and ISIS targets

01:59:37.920 --> 01:59:40.710
to stop them from gaining
any more territory.

01:59:40.710 --> 01:59:42.540
They tried to kill their leadership.

01:59:42.540 --> 01:59:44.160
But the U.S. has to do this carefully

01:59:44.160 --> 01:59:46.620
because they don't want
to engage the Houthis.

01:59:46.620 --> 01:59:48.933
Their fight is with Al-Qaeda and ISIS.

01:59:49.920 --> 01:59:52.680
In May of 2015, the Houthis attack a city

01:59:52.680 --> 01:59:54.840
just across the border in Saudi Arabia,

01:59:54.840 --> 01:59:57.030
killing two and taking hostages,

01:59:57.030 --> 01:59:58.860
forcing an airport to shut down.

01:59:58.860 --> 02:00:00.090
The crown prince doesn't take

02:00:00.090 --> 02:00:02.460
being attacked on his own soil lightly,

02:00:02.460 --> 02:00:04.650
especially by Iran's proxies.

02:00:04.650 --> 02:00:06.330
So over the next 24 hours,

02:00:06.330 --> 02:00:09.540
the Saudis launch 130 airstrikes in Yemen,

02:00:09.540 --> 02:00:12.390
hitting homes, schools, and hospitals.

02:00:12.390 --> 02:00:15.000
Thousands are forced to
flee from their home.

02:00:15.000 --> 02:00:16.650
Hundreds are reported dead,

02:00:16.650 --> 02:00:18.900
though we'll never know
the real death toll.

02:00:18.900 --> 02:00:22.410
These strikes are almost
immediately called war crimes.

02:00:22.410 --> 02:00:24.390
But the crown prince doesn't back down.

02:00:24.390 --> 02:00:27.420
His coalition then sends in
ground troops from the south,

02:00:27.420 --> 02:00:30.480
pushing back the Houthis and
taking back the city of Aden.

02:00:30.480 --> 02:00:34.830
He's behaving as if he's fighting
his greatest enemy, Iran.

02:00:34.830 --> 02:00:38.670
It's a vicious cycle that is
common in proxy wars like this,

02:00:38.670 --> 02:00:40.260
and it lands the people of Yemen

02:00:40.260 --> 02:00:42.780
in the worst humanitarian
crisis in the world.

02:00:42.780 --> 02:00:44.790
The international community
tries to intervene

02:00:44.790 --> 02:00:47.310
with diplomatic efforts
to stop the fighting.

02:00:47.310 --> 02:00:50.100
But over the next six years,
those efforts would fail,

02:00:50.100 --> 02:00:53.130
and Saudi Arabia would
continue its brutal campaign,

02:00:53.130 --> 02:00:55.077
all using American weapons.

02:00:55.077 --> 02:00:57.330
The Houthis continue to fight,

02:00:57.330 --> 02:00:59.070
to show that they are legitimate,

02:00:59.070 --> 02:01:00.840
that they are the government of Yemen.

02:01:00.840 --> 02:01:03.008
They aren't just doing Iran's bidding.

02:01:03.008 --> 02:01:05.400
(somber music)

02:01:05.400 --> 02:01:08.520
The war continues to
destroy cities and land,

02:01:08.520 --> 02:01:11.340
and the blockade continues
to keep trade out.

02:01:11.340 --> 02:01:14.070
The Saudis continue to
bomb indiscriminately.

02:01:14.070 --> 02:01:17.430
And with time, 2 million
Yemenis are displaced,

02:01:17.430 --> 02:01:20.514
forced to leave their homes
to flee this violence.

02:01:20.514 --> 02:01:22.957
- [Reporter] 150,000
civilians have had to-

02:01:22.957 --> 02:01:24.300
- [Johnny] But this
doesn't stop the Houthis

02:01:24.300 --> 02:01:26.070
from escalating their offensive.

02:01:26.070 --> 02:01:28.410
They start attacking Saudi Arabia directly

02:01:28.410 --> 02:01:30.687
with missiles and drones
in their capital city

02:01:30.687 --> 02:01:32.340
and their civilian airports,

02:01:32.340 --> 02:01:34.926
using weapons that most
believe are coming from Iran.

02:01:34.926 --> 02:01:37.980
(tense music)

02:01:37.980 --> 02:01:39.900
In December of 2017,

02:01:39.900 --> 02:01:42.960
the Saudis and the UAE
convinced President Saleh

02:01:42.960 --> 02:01:45.120
to break his alliance with the Houthis

02:01:45.120 --> 02:01:47.430
and return to power as president of Yemen.

02:01:47.430 --> 02:01:49.710
They hope that Saleh, the former dictator,

02:01:49.710 --> 02:01:53.070
might be able to hold onto
power and rule more effectively.

02:01:53.070 --> 02:01:56.430
Saleh takes the deal and
changes sides yet again,

02:01:56.430 --> 02:01:57.690
breaking with the Houthis

02:01:57.690 --> 02:01:59.680
and joining the Saudi-led coalition.

02:01:59.680 --> 02:02:01.380
(reflective music)

02:02:01.380 --> 02:02:03.020
Fighting breaks out between the Houthis

02:02:03.020 --> 02:02:04.980
and Saleh's militias,

02:02:04.980 --> 02:02:07.714
and Saleh is killed just two days later.

02:02:07.714 --> 02:02:11.460
(reflective music)

02:02:11.460 --> 02:02:14.460
Meanwhile, in the United
States, there's a new president.

02:02:14.460 --> 02:02:17.220
President Trump orders
increased drone strikes

02:02:17.220 --> 02:02:19.170
and raids against Al-Qaeda,

02:02:19.170 --> 02:02:20.310
helping government forces

02:02:20.310 --> 02:02:22.620
take back large amounts of territory.

02:02:22.620 --> 02:02:25.860
Despite the war crimes committed
using American weapons,

02:02:25.860 --> 02:02:28.620
Trump approves more weapons
sales to Saudi Arabia

02:02:28.620 --> 02:02:29.823
to wage this war.

02:02:30.810 --> 02:02:33.960
And then in 2018, the conflict
takes on a new dimension.

02:02:33.960 --> 02:02:36.810
The UAE, who's a part of
the Saudi-led coalition,

02:02:36.810 --> 02:02:38.250
is starting to lose faith

02:02:38.250 --> 02:02:40.110
in the direction this war is going.

02:02:40.110 --> 02:02:42.180
They don't like the current
government of Yemen,

02:02:42.180 --> 02:02:44.400
and they're losing soldiers on the ground.

02:02:44.400 --> 02:02:47.280
It's damaging their
reputation on the world stage.

02:02:47.280 --> 02:02:49.830
So the UAE leaves the coalition.

02:02:49.830 --> 02:02:51.660
But they still want to influence

02:02:51.660 --> 02:02:53.580
who will ultimately control Yemen,

02:02:53.580 --> 02:02:56.340
so they start backing a
new group in the south

02:02:56.340 --> 02:02:59.910
called the Southern
Transitional Council, or STC.

02:02:59.910 --> 02:03:03.300
This group isn't aligned with
the government or the Houthis.

02:03:03.300 --> 02:03:05.100
They're calling for a separation

02:03:05.100 --> 02:03:06.600
of the southern part of Yemen.

02:03:06.600 --> 02:03:09.540
They want their own state.
They want their own country.

02:03:09.540 --> 02:03:12.000
So the UAE starts to withdraw their troops

02:03:12.000 --> 02:03:13.380
from the south of the country,

02:03:13.380 --> 02:03:16.380
and the STC separatists
start taking control

02:03:16.380 --> 02:03:17.790
of southern provinces.

02:03:17.790 --> 02:03:20.130
The Saudi coalition is fracturing,

02:03:20.130 --> 02:03:23.370
and yet another group is
jumping into the fighting,

02:03:23.370 --> 02:03:26.853
showing just how up for
grabs Yemen has become.

02:03:29.100 --> 02:03:33.210
By the fall of 2019, the war
has reached maximum complexity.

02:03:33.210 --> 02:03:34.620
You've got the Saudi coalition

02:03:34.620 --> 02:03:36.570
backing the former government of Yemen,

02:03:36.570 --> 02:03:38.940
mostly relegated to the
east of the country.

02:03:38.940 --> 02:03:40.470
The UAE is now backing

02:03:40.470 --> 02:03:42.480
this separatist movement in the south,

02:03:42.480 --> 02:03:45.090
who are fighting against the
former government of Yemen.

02:03:45.090 --> 02:03:49.110
The Houthis, backed by Iran,
still maintain majority control

02:03:49.110 --> 02:03:50.940
of the major cities in the north.

02:03:50.940 --> 02:03:53.640
Al-Qaeda and its militia
allies are still fighting

02:03:53.640 --> 02:03:55.830
to control small slices of the country.

02:03:55.830 --> 02:03:58.170
And the U.S. continues
to conduct airstrikes

02:03:58.170 --> 02:04:00.120
against Al-Qaeda and ISIS,

02:04:00.120 --> 02:04:02.250
while supporting and selling more weapons

02:04:02.250 --> 02:04:03.663
to the Saudi coalition.

02:04:05.130 --> 02:04:07.200
Yemen is fracturing further,

02:04:07.200 --> 02:04:09.330
and if there's any hope for peace,

02:04:09.330 --> 02:04:11.640
power needs to be consolidated.

02:04:11.640 --> 02:04:14.910
So Saudi Arabia and the UAE make amends,

02:04:14.910 --> 02:04:17.820
and then, together, they
convince the government of Yemen

02:04:17.820 --> 02:04:21.090
and the separatists in the
south to form a new coalition

02:04:21.090 --> 02:04:24.480
to fight against the Houthis
together as a united front.

02:04:24.480 --> 02:04:25.913
But it doesn't stop the Houthis,

02:04:25.913 --> 02:04:28.230
who are getting really good at fighting

02:04:28.230 --> 02:04:30.510
and are firing more
Iranian missiles and drones

02:04:30.510 --> 02:04:31.740
into Saudi Arabia,

02:04:31.740 --> 02:04:33.750
hitting state-owned oil refineries

02:04:33.750 --> 02:04:36.960
and threatening to drive
up oil prices globally.

02:04:36.960 --> 02:04:40.950
By January 2021, the
Houthis, a group started

02:04:40.950 --> 02:04:43.980
to provide religious
summer camps to young men,

02:04:43.980 --> 02:04:45.870
are now battle hardened,

02:04:45.870 --> 02:04:49.650
and they control 70 to 80%
of the population of Yemen.

02:04:49.650 --> 02:04:51.930
Later that year, the UN announces

02:04:51.930 --> 02:04:54.690
that approximately 20
million of those people

02:04:54.690 --> 02:04:58.560
are dependent on humanitarian
aid for their daily needs.

02:04:58.560 --> 02:05:01.950
And at this point, the
war starts to settle in

02:05:01.950 --> 02:05:03.991
to an uncomfortable stalemate.

02:05:03.991 --> 02:05:06.270
(somber music)

02:05:06.270 --> 02:05:10.020
In April 2022, the UN brokers a ceasefire,

02:05:10.020 --> 02:05:11.523
and the fighting stops.

02:05:13.350 --> 02:05:14.340
Behind the scenes,

02:05:14.340 --> 02:05:17.820
Saudi Arabia and Iran have
been in diplomatic talks,

02:05:17.820 --> 02:05:19.260
sponsored by China.

02:05:19.260 --> 02:05:21.600
Regional tensions are cooling,

02:05:21.600 --> 02:05:25.980
which means that their proxy
war in Yemen is also cooling.

02:05:25.980 --> 02:05:28.650
Saudi Arabia has had
enough of the fighting.

02:05:28.650 --> 02:05:31.350
President Hadi resigns
and hands power over

02:05:31.350 --> 02:05:34.650
to a council meant to govern
Yemen and manage the war.

02:05:34.650 --> 02:05:37.020
And both sides are tired of the fighting.

02:05:37.020 --> 02:05:40.830
So when that ceasefire is set
to expire in October 2022,

02:05:40.830 --> 02:05:42.840
the conflict mostly stays quiet,

02:05:42.840 --> 02:05:44.520
even without a new agreement.

02:05:44.520 --> 02:05:47.430
By 2023, Saudi Arabia and Iran

02:05:47.430 --> 02:05:50.460
have officially restored
their diplomatic relations.

02:05:50.460 --> 02:05:53.520
Saudi Arabia hoping that this means Iran

02:05:53.520 --> 02:05:55.050
will stop supporting Houthis

02:05:55.050 --> 02:05:56.650
to attack their southern border.

02:05:58.500 --> 02:05:59.550
But the Saudis keep

02:05:59.550 --> 02:06:01.710
their maritime blockade
around the country,

02:06:01.710 --> 02:06:04.710
making life in Yemen
unlivable for millions.

02:06:04.710 --> 02:06:05.610
But at this point,

02:06:05.610 --> 02:06:08.823
the heaviest of the fighting
on all sides has stopped.

02:06:10.140 --> 02:06:12.570
But then in the fall of 2023,

02:06:12.570 --> 02:06:14.490
the Houthis were back in the news.

02:06:14.490 --> 02:06:16.500
After the Palestinian group Hamas

02:06:16.500 --> 02:06:19.140
launches a brutal attack into Israel,

02:06:19.140 --> 02:06:22.380
the Israeli military
invades the Gaza Strip,

02:06:22.380 --> 02:06:24.000
vowing to destroy Hamas

02:06:24.000 --> 02:06:26.130
and free the hostages that they took.

02:06:26.130 --> 02:06:28.440
The campaign leads to tens of thousands

02:06:28.440 --> 02:06:30.510
of Palestinian civilian deaths,

02:06:30.510 --> 02:06:31.920
and it provokes a response

02:06:31.920 --> 02:06:34.740
from Iranian-backed groups
all throughout the region.

02:06:34.740 --> 02:06:36.780
The Houthis are one of those groups.

02:06:36.780 --> 02:06:39.930
They launch drones and cruise
missiles into southern Israel,

02:06:39.930 --> 02:06:41.910
vowing that their attacks won't stop

02:06:41.910 --> 02:06:44.160
until the invasion of Gaza stops.

02:06:44.160 --> 02:06:46.440
And then they start firing
their missiles and drones

02:06:46.440 --> 02:06:49.500
at cargo ships that are
traveling through the Red Sea,

02:06:49.500 --> 02:06:52.920
where 25% of container traffic travels,

02:06:52.920 --> 02:06:55.320
a major artery in our global economy.

02:06:55.320 --> 02:06:57.420
In the days after these
missiles start flying,

02:06:57.420 --> 02:07:01.440
traffic through this Red
Sea route declines by 73%.

02:07:01.440 --> 02:07:05.040
In response, the U.S. and UK
shoot down Houthis missiles

02:07:05.040 --> 02:07:07.893
and drop bombs on Houthis
military targets in Yemen,

02:07:09.540 --> 02:07:11.730
pulling the U.S. and its allies deeper

02:07:11.730 --> 02:07:13.697
into this enduring conflict.

02:07:13.697 --> 02:07:16.680
(reflective music)

02:07:16.680 --> 02:07:18.780
The reason I wanted to tell you the story

02:07:18.780 --> 02:07:20.580
of this conflict in Yemen.

02:07:20.580 --> 02:07:23.220
Because it represents
the vicious cycle present

02:07:23.220 --> 02:07:26.820
in a lot of proxy wars,
where geopolitical rivals

02:07:26.820 --> 02:07:29.370
use their neighbors as battlefields

02:07:29.370 --> 02:07:31.650
instead of fighting each other directly.

02:07:31.650 --> 02:07:34.650
They fund and add fuel to local conflicts,

02:07:34.650 --> 02:07:36.810
escalating them to the entire country,

02:07:36.810 --> 02:07:38.820
and often to the entire region,

02:07:38.820 --> 02:07:41.760
leaving violence and power
vacuums in their wake,

02:07:41.760 --> 02:07:44.100
which are then filled by bad actors

02:07:44.100 --> 02:07:46.440
trying to take advantage
of the instability.

02:07:46.440 --> 02:07:48.480
All of this changes borders,

02:07:48.480 --> 02:07:51.213
destroys cities, and ruins lives.

02:07:52.200 --> 02:07:54.810
It's the story of powerful countries

02:07:54.810 --> 02:07:57.780
and their cynical scramble
for influence and power

02:07:57.780 --> 02:07:59.370
in vulnerable places,

02:07:59.370 --> 02:08:03.483
leaving behind them a wake of
tremendous human suffering.

02:08:03.483 --> 02:08:06.150
(somber music)

02:08:09.779 --> 02:08:10.830
(brooding music)

02:08:10.830 --> 02:08:12.960
- [Reporter] Breaking news.
Because Yemen's Houthis rebels-

02:08:12.960 --> 02:08:15.720
- [Reporter] They could
attack a commercial ship

02:08:15.720 --> 02:08:20.127
and could bog the strait up
for weeks, if not months.

02:08:20.127 --> 02:08:23.580
(protestors chanting in foreign language)

02:08:23.580 --> 02:08:24.810
- [Johnny] There is a part of the world

02:08:24.810 --> 02:08:26.820
that everyone is keeping
an eye on right now.

02:08:26.820 --> 02:08:28.950
- The joint military
strikes were a response

02:08:28.950 --> 02:08:30.720
to nearly two months of attacks

02:08:30.720 --> 02:08:32.850
by the Iran-backed Houthis movement

02:08:32.850 --> 02:08:34.540
on cargo ships in the Red Sea.

02:08:34.540 --> 02:08:37.230
- [Johnny] It is becoming
more and more important

02:08:37.230 --> 02:08:39.750
for the global rivalries and tensions

02:08:39.750 --> 02:08:41.970
that are heating up on
our globe right now.

02:08:41.970 --> 02:08:44.903
It is this choke point in the Red Sea.

02:08:44.903 --> 02:08:47.570
(subdued music)

02:08:49.500 --> 02:08:50.370
It's pretty small.

02:08:50.370 --> 02:08:52.140
It's a few dozen kilometers wide,

02:08:52.140 --> 02:08:53.790
but through this little choke point

02:08:53.790 --> 02:08:56.010
flows a lot of important things.

02:08:56.010 --> 02:08:59.130
The internet, millions of
barrels of oil per day,

02:08:59.130 --> 02:09:02.793
30% of all container ships
that connect global markets.

02:09:04.020 --> 02:09:05.970
And this choke point

02:09:05.970 --> 02:09:09.690
is surrounded by unstable situations.

02:09:09.690 --> 02:09:13.290
Countries that are in
civil wars, rebel militias,

02:09:13.290 --> 02:09:16.560
proxy wars, unstable governments,

02:09:16.560 --> 02:09:19.530
failed states, and pirates.

02:09:19.530 --> 02:09:20.550
In recent decades,

02:09:20.550 --> 02:09:23.220
pirates have hijacked
thousands of ships here

02:09:23.220 --> 02:09:26.370
extracting hundreds of
millions of dollars in ransoms,

02:09:26.370 --> 02:09:28.650
a topic we did a whole deep
dive on in another video.

02:09:28.650 --> 02:09:31.710
And lately, as war has
raged in the Gaza Strip,

02:09:31.710 --> 02:09:35.730
the fiercely anti-Western
Houthis rebels in Yemen

02:09:35.730 --> 02:09:37.620
have been firing rockets at cargo ships

02:09:37.620 --> 02:09:38.910
traveling through this water

02:09:38.910 --> 02:09:41.293
leading the U.S. and UK to strike back.

02:09:41.293 --> 02:09:44.760
- A ship attacked by Iran-backed
Houthis rebels last month

02:09:44.760 --> 02:09:46.110
sank in the Red Sea.

02:09:46.110 --> 02:09:49.800
- [Johnny] The region is vital,
yet volatile all at once.

02:09:49.800 --> 02:09:51.570
And yet in the middle of all of this

02:09:51.570 --> 02:09:54.570
is this one beacon of stability,

02:09:54.570 --> 02:09:57.120
a little piece of land
that is easy to miss,

02:09:57.120 --> 02:09:59.400
but that is more important
than you might think.

02:09:59.400 --> 02:10:01.735
It is the country of Djibouti.

02:10:01.735 --> 02:10:04.235
(tense music)

02:10:06.420 --> 02:10:09.570
Djibouti is this small arid country

02:10:09.570 --> 02:10:11.010
right on the choke point,

02:10:11.010 --> 02:10:14.640
and it is home to the
militaries of the United States

02:10:14.640 --> 02:10:17.400
of several European powers, of Japan,

02:10:17.400 --> 02:10:19.590
Japan's only foreign
military base, by the way,

02:10:19.590 --> 02:10:21.840
and right down the road
from the American base,

02:10:21.840 --> 02:10:25.053
China's first military base
outside of its country.

02:10:28.713 --> 02:10:30.210
(subdued music)

02:10:30.210 --> 02:10:31.530
Qatar has peacekeepers here.

02:10:31.530 --> 02:10:34.110
The United Arab Emirates
ran the biggest port.

02:10:34.110 --> 02:10:37.110
Eritrea seized some of
its territory recently,

02:10:37.110 --> 02:10:40.110
and Ethiopia, one of the
largest countries in Africa,

02:10:40.110 --> 02:10:43.050
runs almost all of its
trade through Djibouti.

02:10:43.050 --> 02:10:44.550
Ethiopia's landlocked, and this is their,

02:10:44.550 --> 02:10:46.170
like, gateway to the world.

02:10:46.170 --> 02:10:48.630
It's a lot. Djibouti is a lot.

02:10:48.630 --> 02:10:49.860
There's a lot going on in Djibouti,

02:10:49.860 --> 02:10:53.310
which is why today we are
talking about this place.

02:10:53.310 --> 02:10:57.540
I want to explore why so
many powers want to be here,

02:10:57.540 --> 02:10:59.070
why everyone is converging

02:10:59.070 --> 02:11:02.790
on this little tiny patch of
land the size of New Jersey.

02:11:02.790 --> 02:11:04.997
And I wanna ask if little Djibouti,

02:11:04.997 --> 02:11:07.320
one of the poorest countries in the world,

02:11:07.320 --> 02:11:11.160
can balance all these rival
powers against each other

02:11:11.160 --> 02:11:12.540
without falling victim to them.

02:11:12.540 --> 02:11:15.660
- [Austin] You know, 10
to 15% of global trade

02:11:15.660 --> 02:11:17.520
moves through the Red Sea.

02:11:17.520 --> 02:11:19.650
We will not hesitate to defend lives

02:11:19.650 --> 02:11:21.150
and the free flow of commerce.

02:11:24.991 --> 02:11:26.100
(subdued music)

02:11:26.100 --> 02:11:29.850
- This little choke point
is called the Bab-el-Mandeb,

02:11:29.850 --> 02:11:32.220
or Gate of Tears.

02:11:32.220 --> 02:11:35.283
It used to be nothing but
a recipe for shipwrecks.

02:11:36.780 --> 02:11:38.970
It was known mostly for strong winds,

02:11:38.970 --> 02:11:41.610
unpredictable crosscurrents
and shallow reefs

02:11:41.610 --> 02:11:44.100
that made navigating it dangerous.

02:11:44.100 --> 02:11:46.050
And it wasn't very valuable

02:11:46.050 --> 02:11:47.130
because if you go through it,

02:11:47.130 --> 02:11:48.450
you just end up in the Red Sea,

02:11:48.450 --> 02:11:51.030
which was a dead-end for most of history.

02:11:51.030 --> 02:11:55.920
That is, until a French company
miraculously cut a canal

02:11:55.920 --> 02:11:57.270
through this part of Egypt

02:11:57.270 --> 02:12:00.540
and changed the world forever,
nearly halving the time

02:12:00.540 --> 02:12:03.240
that it would take for
Europeans to get to Asia

02:12:03.240 --> 02:12:05.940
where they were all racing each
other to conquer, colonize,

02:12:05.940 --> 02:12:08.793
and bring back unfathomable
wealth to their countries.

02:12:14.280 --> 02:12:17.070
The Suez Canal was a massive upgrade

02:12:17.070 --> 02:12:18.693
to this hunt for resources.

02:12:19.845 --> 02:12:21.420
Was this video kind of an excuse

02:12:21.420 --> 02:12:23.670
for me to buy these really
old, beautiful books

02:12:23.670 --> 02:12:24.990
about the Suez Canal?

02:12:24.990 --> 02:12:26.040
Yes, it was.

02:12:26.040 --> 02:12:30.120
For France, this engineering
miracle called the Suez Canal

02:12:30.120 --> 02:12:33.360
was their chance to beat
out their big rival,

02:12:33.360 --> 02:12:36.330
the British Empire, who was
clearly winning the race,

02:12:36.330 --> 02:12:39.420
dominating the routes to
Asia before Suez opened.

02:12:39.420 --> 02:12:41.400
The canal was supposed to change that.

02:12:41.400 --> 02:12:43.350
So now you have a new front in this race,

02:12:43.350 --> 02:12:46.470
a scramble for control over
this new route to Asia.

02:12:46.470 --> 02:12:48.450
This windy little choke point

02:12:48.450 --> 02:12:51.120
was no longer just the Gate of Tears.

02:12:51.120 --> 02:12:53.010
It was an important bit of water,

02:12:53.010 --> 02:12:54.630
one of the most important on earth,

02:12:54.630 --> 02:12:56.010
at least for the empires,

02:12:56.010 --> 02:12:57.723
they had to keep an eye on it.

02:13:00.120 --> 02:13:02.610
So the British take this little island

02:13:02.610 --> 02:13:03.870
right at the choke point.

02:13:03.870 --> 02:13:05.313
They put a lighthouse on it.

02:13:07.800 --> 02:13:08.760
They also take control

02:13:08.760 --> 02:13:10.590
of a port just south of the choke point.

02:13:10.590 --> 02:13:11.940
The Italians then show up

02:13:11.940 --> 02:13:14.880
and purchase this bit of
coastline from local sultans

02:13:14.880 --> 02:13:15.900
eventually taking over

02:13:15.900 --> 02:13:18.210
this whole stretch of
coast called Eritrea.

02:13:18.210 --> 02:13:20.640
And the French would start fixating

02:13:20.640 --> 02:13:23.280
on this strip of sparsely populated coast

02:13:23.280 --> 02:13:24.840
right on the banks of the choke point,

02:13:24.840 --> 02:13:27.213
the land that would become Djibouti.

02:13:29.040 --> 02:13:31.080
The sultans here told the French

02:13:31.080 --> 02:13:32.370
that, yes, they could have a bit

02:13:32.370 --> 02:13:36.090
of this newly strategic land
in exchange for protection.

02:13:36.090 --> 02:13:38.190
The locals here knew what was going on.

02:13:38.190 --> 02:13:40.740
The great empires were
all scrambling and racing,

02:13:40.740 --> 02:13:43.770
and they were going to use
their prime real estate

02:13:43.770 --> 02:13:47.220
to get what they wanted
to leverage the situation.

02:13:47.220 --> 02:13:50.343
Smart and totally a theme
throughout this video.

02:13:53.040 --> 02:13:54.720
So the French set up their colony,

02:13:54.720 --> 02:13:57.810
they call it Cotes Francaises des Somalies

02:13:57.810 --> 02:13:59.220
or French Somaliland.

02:13:59.220 --> 02:14:02.100
This choke point would become a key base

02:14:02.100 --> 02:14:04.260
for projecting power and refueling

02:14:04.260 --> 02:14:06.629
on their way to carve up and plunder Asia.

02:14:06.629 --> 02:14:09.720
(intense music)

02:14:09.720 --> 02:14:10.920
France would hold on

02:14:10.920 --> 02:14:14.130
to their little colonial
invention for a long time.

02:14:14.130 --> 02:14:17.430
Even as the world would
decolonize after World War II,

02:14:17.430 --> 02:14:18.720
France held on

02:14:18.720 --> 02:14:21.420
to this strategic colony
along the choke point

02:14:21.420 --> 02:14:23.970
until eventually the locals rose up.

02:14:23.970 --> 02:14:24.930
There were protests,

02:14:24.930 --> 02:14:26.790
they clashed with the local authorities,

02:14:26.790 --> 02:14:28.890
and it led to an independence vote.

02:14:28.890 --> 02:14:32.913
And in 1977, Djibouti
became a new country.

02:14:40.140 --> 02:14:43.590
Djibouti was independent,
but they had a problem.

02:14:43.590 --> 02:14:47.160
In the words of some "New York
Times" reporting from 1977,

02:14:47.160 --> 02:14:49.200
the tiny new nation had no army,

02:14:49.200 --> 02:14:51.510
less than one square mile of arable land,

02:14:51.510 --> 02:14:52.680
and no resources

02:14:52.680 --> 02:14:57.240
except for sand, salt, and 20,000 camels.

02:14:57.240 --> 02:14:58.320
Yeah, not good.

02:14:58.320 --> 02:15:01.530
So once again, they
made a deal with France.

02:15:01.530 --> 02:15:03.000
Djibouti wouldn't be France's anymore,

02:15:03.000 --> 02:15:03.930
but they would be allowed

02:15:03.930 --> 02:15:06.810
to retain a military base in the country

02:15:06.810 --> 02:15:09.090
in exchange for security and protection

02:15:09.090 --> 02:15:10.530
as well as economic aid.

02:15:10.530 --> 02:15:11.760
But this didn't change the fact

02:15:11.760 --> 02:15:14.010
that Djibouti had very
little natural resources

02:15:14.010 --> 02:15:14.910
or arable land.

02:15:14.910 --> 02:15:17.400
Their economy fell into
a crippling depression.

02:15:17.400 --> 02:15:18.930
Eventually there was a civil war

02:15:18.930 --> 02:15:20.632
and it wasn't looking good for Djibouti.

02:15:20.632 --> 02:15:23.250
Djibouti's salvation would eventually come

02:15:23.250 --> 02:15:25.560
when they realized that
they should harken back

02:15:25.560 --> 02:15:28.410
to the old days and start
exploiting the thing

02:15:28.410 --> 02:15:31.290
that had made them valuable
all those years ago:

02:15:31.290 --> 02:15:33.240
Their location.

02:15:33.240 --> 02:15:35.430
- [Reporter] Let's go. Go, go, go, go!

02:15:35.430 --> 02:15:36.630
Stop shooting, man. Go!

02:15:36.630 --> 02:15:38.498
- A plane has crashed
into the World Trade-

02:15:38.498 --> 02:15:39.493
- [Reporter] Can you tell
us how this happened?

02:15:39.493 --> 02:15:40.326
- Not right now.

02:15:40.326 --> 02:15:42.500
- Our very freedom came under attack

02:15:42.500 --> 02:15:46.350
in a series of deliberate
and deadly terrorist acts.

02:15:46.350 --> 02:15:48.210
- [Johnny] Three months after 9/11,

02:15:48.210 --> 02:15:50.970
the United States was
about to come crashing

02:15:50.970 --> 02:15:52.740
into the Middle East in a new way,

02:15:52.740 --> 02:15:55.830
and they asked Djibouti if
they could set up a base there.

02:15:55.830 --> 02:15:57.600
And Djibouti says yes.

02:15:57.600 --> 02:15:59.520
- Suddenly after the global war on terror,

02:15:59.520 --> 02:16:03.540
America realizes that it's
one of the key locations.

02:16:03.540 --> 02:16:05.250
It matters to be there.

02:16:05.250 --> 02:16:07.740
You had, you know, Al-Qaeda in Yemen,

02:16:07.740 --> 02:16:10.710
you had Al-Qaeda and even Horn of Africa.

02:16:10.710 --> 02:16:12.180
So they needed a base.

02:16:12.180 --> 02:16:13.710
And Djibouti was one country

02:16:13.710 --> 02:16:15.060
which was willing to offer it.

02:16:15.060 --> 02:16:17.490
- It would become America's
only permanent military base

02:16:17.490 --> 02:16:18.630
on the continent of Africa.

02:16:18.630 --> 02:16:20.130
And it would be a key jumping-off point

02:16:20.130 --> 02:16:22.740
for George Bush's war on terror.

02:16:22.740 --> 02:16:26.070
And reminder that it's
now the 21st century.

02:16:26.070 --> 02:16:27.570
Things have changed in the world.

02:16:27.570 --> 02:16:28.920
And this little choke point

02:16:28.920 --> 02:16:31.050
was becoming more valuable again,

02:16:31.050 --> 02:16:33.349
but for different reasons
than the old days.

02:16:33.349 --> 02:16:36.120
(ship horn blares)

02:16:36.120 --> 02:16:40.710
Number one, the world had
become addicted to fossil fuels,

02:16:40.710 --> 02:16:43.620
oil and gas, which was the
lifeblood of their economies.

02:16:43.620 --> 02:16:46.980
And most of that oil and gas
was coming from over here,

02:16:46.980 --> 02:16:48.470
from the land and waters

02:16:48.470 --> 02:16:51.300
of the countries around this Persian Gulf

02:16:51.300 --> 02:16:55.590
where it was then put on ships
and sent around the world,

02:16:55.590 --> 02:16:58.980
a lot of it heading west
through this vital choke point

02:16:58.980 --> 02:17:02.730
up the Red Sea into the
Suez Canal and onto Europe.

02:17:02.730 --> 02:17:04.890
The world knew that their
energy was vulnerable

02:17:04.890 --> 02:17:07.110
because Egypt in earlier decades

02:17:07.110 --> 02:17:11.070
had weaponized the canal by
closing it, by blocking it.

02:17:11.070 --> 02:17:13.020
One time for eight years,

02:17:13.020 --> 02:17:15.180
inflicting immediate pain on the countries

02:17:15.180 --> 02:17:19.113
that rely on this constant
flow of energy and goods.

02:17:20.100 --> 02:17:21.840
By the way, semi side note,

02:17:21.840 --> 02:17:24.390
this flow of energy
becomes even more important

02:17:24.390 --> 02:17:26.550
when Europe almost entirely cuts ties

02:17:26.550 --> 02:17:27.690
with Russian natural gas

02:17:27.690 --> 02:17:29.810
after Russia invaded Ukraine in 2022.

02:17:29.810 --> 02:17:32.340
So they have to get more
natural gas from this region.

02:17:32.340 --> 02:17:34.860
Qatar was not sad about
that, let's put it that way.

02:17:34.860 --> 02:17:36.300
Anyway, we're getting off topic.

02:17:36.300 --> 02:17:38.910
Europe needs this route to stay stable.

02:17:38.910 --> 02:17:41.110
They need these little
dots to keep flowing.

02:17:43.080 --> 02:17:44.550
So that's one reason that the choke point

02:17:44.550 --> 02:17:45.600
has become more vital than ever.

02:17:45.600 --> 02:17:47.250
The other reason is China.

02:17:47.250 --> 02:17:49.680
(subdued music)

02:17:49.680 --> 02:17:53.490
China rose to become a
manufacturing superpower.

02:17:53.490 --> 02:17:56.370
They make a lot of stuff and
they send it to the West.

02:17:56.370 --> 02:17:59.220
And a lot of that comes
through this choke point.

02:17:59.220 --> 02:18:01.470
And all of this vital trade is happening

02:18:01.470 --> 02:18:04.800
in a region that is full
of unstable governments,

02:18:04.800 --> 02:18:08.730
rising anti-Western
militias and Somali pirates

02:18:08.730 --> 02:18:11.730
making this route precarious and dangerous

02:18:11.730 --> 02:18:13.533
and subject to problems.

02:18:16.050 --> 02:18:19.230
So Djibouti has become this vital place

02:18:19.230 --> 02:18:20.430
here at the choke point,

02:18:20.430 --> 02:18:22.890
an incredibly important strategic location

02:18:22.890 --> 02:18:24.480
in a sea of turmoil.

02:18:24.480 --> 02:18:25.530
Okay, but there's more.

02:18:25.530 --> 02:18:28.170
In addition to all of this
global economy importance,

02:18:28.170 --> 02:18:30.600
there's also regional
importance to Djibouti.

02:18:30.600 --> 02:18:33.000
Notice Djibouti's neighbor, Ethiopia,

02:18:33.000 --> 02:18:35.700
the second most populous
country in Africa,

02:18:35.700 --> 02:18:39.840
and as you can see, one
that is totally landlocked.

02:18:39.840 --> 02:18:43.470
It is not good to be a big
country that is landlocked

02:18:43.470 --> 02:18:45.600
because the water is where we trade.

02:18:45.600 --> 02:18:48.060
So Djibouti is the gateway

02:18:48.060 --> 02:18:51.360
to Ethiopia's millions of
people to trade with the world.

02:18:51.360 --> 02:18:55.410
95% of Ethiopia's trade
passes through Djibouti.

02:18:55.410 --> 02:18:59.850
So Djibouti, this colonial
creation from France in the 1880s

02:18:59.850 --> 02:19:04.800
is now once again an incredibly
important place on the map.

02:19:04.800 --> 02:19:08.101
It's become, once again, a
highly strategic location.

02:19:08.101 --> 02:19:10.770
(inquisitive music)

02:19:10.770 --> 02:19:13.230
And when there are highly
strategic locations,

02:19:13.230 --> 02:19:14.460
superpowers start moving in.

02:19:14.460 --> 02:19:17.910
In 2011, Djibouti approved Japan,

02:19:17.910 --> 02:19:21.000
a pacifist sort of non-military country,

02:19:21.000 --> 02:19:24.120
to open a military base on Djibouti,

02:19:24.120 --> 02:19:26.170
their one and only foreign military base.

02:19:27.420 --> 02:19:30.840
Then you have Italy who set up
a military base here in 2013.

02:19:30.840 --> 02:19:32.730
Spain and Germany also have
a military presence here.

02:19:32.730 --> 02:19:34.853
They're not permanently
hosted with a base,

02:19:34.853 --> 02:19:36.930
they're actually hosted by France,

02:19:36.930 --> 02:19:41.370
and the soldiers live in
these hotels permanently.

02:19:41.370 --> 02:19:42.450
Okay. That's not weird at all.

02:19:42.450 --> 02:19:45.660
But yeah, as you can see,
the great powers are back.

02:19:45.660 --> 02:19:47.490
They're back in Djibouti
like the old days,

02:19:47.490 --> 02:19:50.280
not to compete with one another,
but to all band together

02:19:50.280 --> 02:19:53.610
to police this vital choke
point so that the global economy

02:19:53.610 --> 02:19:56.550
that has brought prosperity
to all of us can stay stable.

02:19:56.550 --> 02:19:59.190
Everything keeps flowing.
Everyone is happy.

02:19:59.190 --> 02:20:02.100
And this solved a big
problem for Djibouti,

02:20:02.100 --> 02:20:04.410
who, again, didn't have a big industry

02:20:04.410 --> 02:20:05.940
or any kind of resource.

02:20:05.940 --> 02:20:07.830
They make a lot of money
off of these leases

02:20:07.830 --> 02:20:11.040
to foreign countries, which
has helped build their economy.

02:20:11.040 --> 02:20:14.730
And like the old days,
when one side shows up,

02:20:14.730 --> 02:20:16.796
the rival won't be far behind.

02:20:16.796 --> 02:20:19.296
(tense music)

02:20:21.120 --> 02:20:24.020
- China has agreed to set up
a support base in Djibouti.

02:20:24.020 --> 02:20:26.250
- So the Chinese base is right here,

02:20:26.250 --> 02:20:29.310
and they're gonna be watching
us as we are watching them.

02:20:29.310 --> 02:20:31.380
- In 2016, Djibouti announces

02:20:31.380 --> 02:20:33.540
that they've been talking to China

02:20:33.540 --> 02:20:35.580
and that they've agreed to give them land

02:20:35.580 --> 02:20:37.590
to open their own military base,

02:20:37.590 --> 02:20:40.050
which was built and
opened right down the road

02:20:40.050 --> 02:20:42.750
from the American base in 2017.

02:20:42.750 --> 02:20:45.390
So China says they're
there because of pirates.

02:20:45.390 --> 02:20:47.760
Pirates have been wreaking
havoc in this area.

02:20:47.760 --> 02:20:50.640
So they're there to stabilize
their economic interests

02:20:50.640 --> 02:20:51.473
because of pirates.

02:20:51.473 --> 02:20:53.970
But as we discovered in
our deep dive on pirates,

02:20:53.970 --> 02:20:57.660
by 2017, the pirate
issue was basically over.

02:20:57.660 --> 02:20:59.730
There was almost no
hijackings in this region.

02:20:59.730 --> 02:21:00.960
I mean, look at this graph.

02:21:00.960 --> 02:21:04.650
Oh, and the Chinese base
has this big deep pier

02:21:04.650 --> 02:21:08.700
that is like large enough to
handle an aircraft carrier

02:21:08.700 --> 02:21:10.230
and nuclear submarines.

02:21:10.230 --> 02:21:12.300
So this isn't about pirates.

02:21:12.300 --> 02:21:14.880
China also did the most China
thing when they moved in here,

02:21:14.880 --> 02:21:18.060
which was instead of just
building a base, they also came

02:21:18.060 --> 02:21:20.880
with all of these big-ticket
infrastructure projects

02:21:20.880 --> 02:21:24.600
as like gifts to win
the favor of the locals.

02:21:24.600 --> 02:21:29.130
We're talking railway, pipeline,
port, all very expensive,

02:21:29.130 --> 02:21:32.070
all putting Djibouti into
a lot of debt to China.

02:21:32.070 --> 02:21:33.720
They're doing this all over Africa,

02:21:33.720 --> 02:21:36.030
which we talk about in
another deep dive video

02:21:36.030 --> 02:21:37.770
we did on the topic mapping all

02:21:37.770 --> 02:21:40.500
of China's giant infrastructure
projects in Africa

02:21:40.500 --> 02:21:41.490
and what that means.

02:21:41.490 --> 02:21:43.350
Man, a lot of shout outs
to other videos today, huh?

02:21:43.350 --> 02:21:45.180
So Djibouti is now the only place on earth

02:21:45.180 --> 02:21:49.020
where the United States and
China, these two rivals,

02:21:49.020 --> 02:21:51.900
have military bases
right next to each other.

02:21:51.900 --> 02:21:54.840
And so far everyone's been playing nice.

02:21:54.840 --> 02:21:55.980
There's been no shenanigans

02:21:55.980 --> 02:21:57.570
other than both sides accusing each other

02:21:57.570 --> 02:21:59.430
of spying on each other constantly.

02:21:59.430 --> 02:22:01.110
Oh, and there was the lasers.

02:22:01.110 --> 02:22:03.540
The U.S. says that China
was pointing lasers

02:22:03.540 --> 02:22:06.810
into the eyes of pilots
while they were taking off,

02:22:06.810 --> 02:22:09.420
which like if they really did
that, that's just like, why?

02:22:09.420 --> 02:22:10.560
Why would you do that?

02:22:10.560 --> 02:22:12.330
Like, why did you do that? Don't do that.

02:22:12.330 --> 02:22:15.240
Okay, but Djibouti doesn't
let every country in.

02:22:15.240 --> 02:22:17.850
Russia wanted to have
their own base in Djibouti.

02:22:17.850 --> 02:22:19.470
And Djibouti said no.

02:22:19.470 --> 02:22:20.490
It probably helped

02:22:20.490 --> 02:22:23.700
that the American national
security advisor flew to Djibouti

02:22:23.700 --> 02:22:24.990
and told them that the U.S.

02:22:24.990 --> 02:22:27.270
would double how much they're
paying for their lease

02:22:27.270 --> 02:22:29.228
if Djibouti didn't let Russia in.

02:22:29.228 --> 02:22:32.430
(gentle music)

02:22:32.430 --> 02:22:34.290
Okay, but what about the people?

02:22:34.290 --> 02:22:36.510
Djibouti isn't just one big military base.

02:22:36.510 --> 02:22:39.060
There's almost a million
people who live here

02:22:39.060 --> 02:22:41.790
and their day-to-day probably consists

02:22:41.790 --> 02:22:44.790
of a lot of jet engine sounds.

02:22:44.790 --> 02:22:46.830
I got to talk to my old grad
school professor, David Vine,

02:22:46.830 --> 02:22:49.080
who is an expert on U.S. military bases

02:22:49.080 --> 02:22:50.940
and how they affect the locals.

02:22:50.940 --> 02:22:54.990
- So what we were talking
about is a foreign government,

02:22:54.990 --> 02:22:59.640
foreign military occupying large
swaths of land, fenced off,

02:22:59.640 --> 02:23:03.060
and hosting large amounts of weaponry,

02:23:03.060 --> 02:23:05.340
high-powered weaponry of many, many kinds.

02:23:05.340 --> 02:23:09.590
(Hassan speaking foreign language)

02:23:13.590 --> 02:23:17.520
- Because these bases are so
close to like the big city,

02:23:17.520 --> 02:23:21.000
there have been accidents,
especially with drones.

02:23:21.000 --> 02:23:23.460
The U.S. used to take off their
drones and some would fall

02:23:23.460 --> 02:23:26.340
and actually crash onto like
civilian infrastructure.

02:23:26.340 --> 02:23:28.119
So they had to open a separate airstrip

02:23:28.119 --> 02:23:32.452
(Hassan speaking foreign language)

02:23:40.800 --> 02:23:43.800
- Apart from occasionally
running over cows,

02:23:43.800 --> 02:23:47.250
the soldiers mostly keep to themselves.

02:23:47.250 --> 02:23:50.070
U.S. soldiers don't even go
into the city to like buy stuff.

02:23:50.070 --> 02:23:51.540
All of it is flown in

02:23:51.540 --> 02:23:54.600
creating a little slice of
America right in Djibouti.

02:23:54.600 --> 02:23:57.240
Like, they don't even drink
the water from Djibouti.

02:23:57.240 --> 02:23:58.254
It's all flown in.

02:23:58.254 --> 02:24:02.504
(Hassan speaking foreign language)

02:24:17.050 --> 02:24:19.335
- And then there's the dictator.

02:24:19.335 --> 02:24:20.794
- Well, I wanna welcome President Guelleh

02:24:20.794 --> 02:24:22.329
and his delegation here today.

02:24:22.329 --> 02:24:23.640
- The U.S. base in particular,

02:24:23.640 --> 02:24:25.770
but also the French and Chinese bases,

02:24:25.770 --> 02:24:28.350
which are the largest of the collection,

02:24:28.350 --> 02:24:31.560
are all helping to prop
up undemocratic regime

02:24:31.560 --> 02:24:33.510
that has, you know, been accused

02:24:33.510 --> 02:24:35.760
of widespread human rights abuses

02:24:35.760 --> 02:24:39.120
- Every time a foreign power
makes a deal with Djibouti

02:24:39.120 --> 02:24:40.140
and pays them a lot of money

02:24:40.140 --> 02:24:42.480
in exchange for them
being able to be there,

02:24:42.480 --> 02:24:45.690
they basically validate
this terrible dictator

02:24:45.690 --> 02:24:47.820
who's been in power for 25 years.

02:24:47.820 --> 02:24:51.030
There's no real elections,
free press, freedom of speech,

02:24:51.030 --> 02:24:53.310
plenty of human rights abuses.

02:24:53.310 --> 02:24:56.130
But on the other side,
there's at least one way

02:24:56.130 --> 02:24:59.130
that regular people in Djibouti
benefit from all of this.

02:24:59.130 --> 02:25:01.200
Fewer people go hungry today

02:25:01.200 --> 02:25:03.120
than at the conclusion of the civil war.

02:25:03.120 --> 02:25:04.800
And even though the economy is still poor,

02:25:04.800 --> 02:25:07.500
all of this foreign aid
still does support it.

02:25:07.500 --> 02:25:10.170
Is that worth it to them?
I don't actually know.

02:25:10.170 --> 02:25:13.005
And another version of this
video that I wanna do someday

02:25:13.005 --> 02:25:15.000
is to go there and talk to the people

02:25:15.000 --> 02:25:18.360
and hear what their thoughts
are about the bases in Djibouti

02:25:18.360 --> 02:25:19.710
and what that feels like

02:25:19.710 --> 02:25:22.380
to live so close to all of
these foreign militaries.

02:25:22.380 --> 02:25:23.213
For now, we're talking

02:25:23.213 --> 02:25:25.912
about the geography, the
history, the geopolitics.

02:25:25.912 --> 02:25:28.495
(upbeat music)

02:25:29.880 --> 02:25:31.920
Okay, speaking of geopolitics,

02:25:31.920 --> 02:25:34.470
there is another angle to this story

02:25:34.470 --> 02:25:37.710
that is devilishly
complicated to get into.

02:25:37.710 --> 02:25:39.660
And I'm not gonna get into
all the juicy details.

02:25:39.660 --> 02:25:41.460
You'll understand why when
I start talking about it.

02:25:41.460 --> 02:25:45.030
It has to do with the regional
geopolitics, the rivalries,

02:25:45.030 --> 02:25:46.410
and the concerns and the issues

02:25:46.410 --> 02:25:49.050
of all of these countries that
are right next to Djibouti,

02:25:49.050 --> 02:25:50.340
not just the outside powers.

02:25:50.340 --> 02:25:51.630
I want to just give you a little sampling

02:25:51.630 --> 02:25:53.580
of what this looks like
because it's pretty wild.

02:25:53.580 --> 02:25:55.320
So you have all these Gulf countries,

02:25:55.320 --> 02:25:58.740
and some of them are not
totally on the same team,

02:25:58.740 --> 02:26:02.010
but almost all of them are
thinking about the future

02:26:02.010 --> 02:26:03.240
and they're realizing that oil

02:26:03.240 --> 02:26:05.040
is not gonna be a big part of their future

02:26:05.040 --> 02:26:06.120
because oil is not gonna be

02:26:06.120 --> 02:26:07.980
a big part of any of our future hopefully.

02:26:07.980 --> 02:26:10.050
And they're realizing
that they're going to need

02:26:10.050 --> 02:26:13.440
to invest in the Horn of
Africa, this region here,

02:26:13.440 --> 02:26:15.330
which they believe is
probably gonna be the future

02:26:15.330 --> 02:26:17.880
of their food, their future bread basket.

02:26:17.880 --> 02:26:19.350
So hold that for a second

02:26:19.350 --> 02:26:21.930
and then look at Ethiopia and Eritrea.

02:26:21.930 --> 02:26:23.310
Ethiopia, as we mentioned,

02:26:23.310 --> 02:26:25.470
uses Djibouti as their
gateway to the world.

02:26:25.470 --> 02:26:27.750
They need it to feed
their millions of people.

02:26:27.750 --> 02:26:31.620
All of this geopolitics is
also playing out in Djibouti,

02:26:31.620 --> 02:26:32.550
this little country

02:26:32.550 --> 02:26:35.040
that has to navigate everyone's interests.

02:26:35.040 --> 02:26:36.870
So here's a quick episode of this drama

02:26:36.870 --> 02:26:38.283
to give you an idea of what it looks like.

02:26:38.283 --> 02:26:40.200
(intense music)

02:26:40.200 --> 02:26:42.720
The United Arab Emirates, UAE,

02:26:42.720 --> 02:26:44.870
used to be like best
friends with Djibouti.

02:26:44.870 --> 02:26:47.340
It was like one of their
most important partners.

02:26:47.340 --> 02:26:50.430
The UAE built and owned this giant port

02:26:50.430 --> 02:26:53.220
that basically runs
Djibouti's entire economy.

02:26:53.220 --> 02:26:56.580
But then the UAE got really
close to Djibouti's neighbor

02:26:56.580 --> 02:26:59.010
and kind of enemy Eritrea.

02:26:59.010 --> 02:27:00.360
This was very worrisome

02:27:00.360 --> 02:27:02.430
because Djibouti doesn't want Eritrea

02:27:02.430 --> 02:27:03.900
to become the new Djibouti.

02:27:03.900 --> 02:27:05.130
They're a mortal threat.

02:27:05.130 --> 02:27:07.560
They don't want the UAE to
just shut down Djibouti's,

02:27:07.560 --> 02:27:09.900
like, strategic offering.

02:27:09.900 --> 02:27:12.660
Okay, fast forward to like 2017,

02:27:12.660 --> 02:27:15.480
the UAE with their close
friend, Saudi Arabia,

02:27:15.480 --> 02:27:17.520
are in a feud with Qatar,

02:27:17.520 --> 02:27:21.360
which also has a military
presence in Djibouti

02:27:21.360 --> 02:27:22.950
right up here on this border.

02:27:22.950 --> 02:27:27.240
Now that Qatar and the UAE
are fighting with each other,

02:27:27.240 --> 02:27:28.290
they turn to Djibouti

02:27:28.290 --> 02:27:31.650
and say, "Hey, you are
too close with the UAE.

02:27:31.650 --> 02:27:32.580
We're out of here."

02:27:32.580 --> 02:27:36.330
Qatar takes their soldiers
out of this border area,

02:27:36.330 --> 02:27:37.470
and right away,

02:27:37.470 --> 02:27:40.290
Eritrea takes some of
this land from Djibouti.

02:27:40.290 --> 02:27:43.200
This may seem like some
petty border skirmishes,

02:27:43.200 --> 02:27:45.210
but remember where we are here.

02:27:45.210 --> 02:27:48.960
This is the vital choke
point, Bab-el-Mandeb,

02:27:48.960 --> 02:27:51.240
every inch of land matters.

02:27:51.240 --> 02:27:52.590
Every little conflict

02:27:52.590 --> 02:27:55.080
could be the spark that
stops the global economy.

02:27:55.080 --> 02:27:56.820
And what this little flare up shows us

02:27:56.820 --> 02:27:58.740
is how Djibouti finds itself

02:27:58.740 --> 02:28:01.410
in the center of conflicts
between other countries

02:28:01.410 --> 02:28:03.210
that it kind of doesn't
want to be in the middle of,

02:28:03.210 --> 02:28:06.060
but, like, has found
itself in the middle of

02:28:06.060 --> 02:28:08.460
because it hosts all of their militaries.

02:28:08.460 --> 02:28:09.660
But wait, there's more.

02:28:09.660 --> 02:28:11.820
Djibouti is actually really good at this.

02:28:11.820 --> 02:28:13.410
They know how to play the game

02:28:13.410 --> 02:28:15.120
and to play all these
powers off of each other

02:28:15.120 --> 02:28:16.650
and we'll see what happens next.

02:28:16.650 --> 02:28:18.567
So they're kind of beholden to the UAE,

02:28:18.567 --> 02:28:20.640
UAE has way too much leverage over them.

02:28:20.640 --> 02:28:21.960
So finally it all comes to a head

02:28:21.960 --> 02:28:26.220
and they kick the UAE out
from this really vital port,

02:28:26.220 --> 02:28:30.810
and they give a massive chunk
of it to, wait for it, China,

02:28:30.810 --> 02:28:33.780
the new arrival to Djibouti
that no one wants to mess with.

02:28:33.780 --> 02:28:35.550
They now have the port

02:28:35.550 --> 02:28:37.620
and Djibouti is now on their side

02:28:37.620 --> 02:28:39.540
and the UAE can't do anything about it.

02:28:39.540 --> 02:28:42.540
Djibouti saved itself from one power

02:28:42.540 --> 02:28:44.610
by pleasing a bigger power.

02:28:44.610 --> 02:28:45.810
Do you see how this works?

02:28:45.810 --> 02:28:47.439
Do you see what game they're playing here?

02:28:47.439 --> 02:28:50.370
(subdued music)

02:28:50.370 --> 02:28:54.560
So that is why this tiny
country in East Africa

02:28:54.560 --> 02:28:56.130
is so important.

02:28:56.130 --> 02:28:59.820
It's kind of the
definition of geopolitics,

02:28:59.820 --> 02:29:02.640
politics happening because of geography.

02:29:02.640 --> 02:29:04.500
To project legitimacy.

02:29:04.500 --> 02:29:05.910
This choke point is only going

02:29:05.910 --> 02:29:07.740
to become more important.

02:29:07.740 --> 02:29:09.360
Day by day, we're seeing it happen.

02:29:09.360 --> 02:29:10.857
I mean, like right now
as I'm filming this,

02:29:10.857 --> 02:29:11.850
the Houthis rebels

02:29:11.850 --> 02:29:14.370
are like potentially
cutting internet cables

02:29:14.370 --> 02:29:15.240
in this choke point.

02:29:15.240 --> 02:29:17.181
- Three crew members have
been killed in a Houthis-

02:29:17.181 --> 02:29:18.660
- The first death since Houthis rebels

02:29:18.660 --> 02:29:20.880
began attacking shipping in the Red Sea.

02:29:20.880 --> 02:29:23.250
- After months of bombing merchant ships

02:29:23.250 --> 02:29:27.420
to punish the West for
supporting the IDF war in Gaza.

02:29:27.420 --> 02:29:28.740
I mean, it's this kind of threat

02:29:28.740 --> 02:29:30.540
that Djibouti was set up to handle.

02:29:30.540 --> 02:29:32.580
That is why all these
foreign militaries are here.

02:29:32.580 --> 02:29:36.210
And yet for me, there is a subtler threat

02:29:36.210 --> 02:29:38.040
that is emerging here,

02:29:38.040 --> 02:29:41.733
which you can see when you
just look at this map of bases.

02:29:43.440 --> 02:29:46.320
Djibouti hosts rival countries

02:29:46.320 --> 02:29:48.900
during a time when we
are transitioning away

02:29:48.900 --> 02:29:52.590
from a rules-based order
where all the great powers

02:29:52.590 --> 02:29:55.560
have an incentive to work
together under one system

02:29:55.560 --> 02:29:57.270
that is dominated by the United States

02:29:57.270 --> 02:29:59.910
that deeply benefits the
United States and its allies.

02:29:59.910 --> 02:30:02.370
But that works because the United States

02:30:02.370 --> 02:30:05.460
imposes and protects those rules.

02:30:05.460 --> 02:30:07.140
Those rules are being challenged.

02:30:07.140 --> 02:30:10.320
And what is emerging is
a system that is messier,

02:30:10.320 --> 02:30:13.020
more disruptive to these reliable rules

02:30:13.020 --> 02:30:14.730
that have kept everything stable,

02:30:14.730 --> 02:30:16.260
kept the United States on top.

02:30:16.260 --> 02:30:18.780
And unfortunately we know all too well

02:30:18.780 --> 02:30:21.030
what happens when great powers

02:30:21.030 --> 02:30:24.270
start competing with
each other more and more

02:30:24.270 --> 02:30:28.140
over vital resources
and vital geographies.

02:30:28.140 --> 02:30:30.840
In fact, most of the history of war

02:30:30.840 --> 02:30:33.300
is what happens when great powers

02:30:33.300 --> 02:30:34.620
are competing with each other.

02:30:34.620 --> 02:30:37.410
It's ugly, it's messy, it's violent.

02:30:37.410 --> 02:30:40.530
It provokes a zero-sum way of thinking,

02:30:40.530 --> 02:30:44.790
competition racing to secure and control

02:30:44.790 --> 02:30:49.380
and exploit vital geographies
like strategic choke points

02:30:49.380 --> 02:30:52.560
that lead into the most
important waterway on the planet.

02:30:52.560 --> 02:30:54.210
As this rivalry heats up,

02:30:54.210 --> 02:30:57.300
Djibouti's role is going
to become more complex.

02:30:57.300 --> 02:30:59.940
Its government will continue
to do what it's done,

02:30:59.940 --> 02:31:01.620
playing the big powers off each other,

02:31:01.620 --> 02:31:04.650
exploiting its great real
estate to benefit itself.

02:31:04.650 --> 02:31:06.600
But I believe that there comes a point

02:31:06.600 --> 02:31:08.700
in every escalating conflict

02:31:08.700 --> 02:31:11.250
where you kind of have to choose sides.

02:31:11.250 --> 02:31:13.050
I want Djibouti to continue

02:31:13.050 --> 02:31:16.410
to embody the positive sum thinking

02:31:16.410 --> 02:31:18.930
that has kept the world relatively stable

02:31:18.930 --> 02:31:20.700
these last 70 years.

02:31:20.700 --> 02:31:22.290
But it's definitely playing

02:31:22.290 --> 02:31:24.540
with a very delicate balancing act

02:31:24.540 --> 02:31:26.550
and there's a chance
that it could stumble.

02:31:26.550 --> 02:31:29.340
So as you all know, I
will be keeping an eye

02:31:29.340 --> 02:31:33.420
on the global order as well as Djibouti

02:31:33.420 --> 02:31:35.430
to try to see where this is going,

02:31:35.430 --> 02:31:36.510
to see if the great powers

02:31:36.510 --> 02:31:40.110
can continue to cooperate
and compete, but cooperate.

02:31:40.110 --> 02:31:42.060
Or if we're heading back into the old days

02:31:42.060 --> 02:31:45.360
where competition threatens
to rip the world apart.

02:31:45.360 --> 02:31:46.590
I don't think it's inevitable,

02:31:46.590 --> 02:31:48.540
but it's gonna take effort to avoid it.

02:31:51.184 --> 02:31:53.851
(subdued music)

02:31:56.070 --> 02:31:58.593
I've been sleeping every
night in the desert.

02:32:01.740 --> 02:32:04.623
Up here in the northwest
corner of Saudi Arabia.

02:32:05.759 --> 02:32:08.426
(weighty music)

02:32:09.870 --> 02:32:11.073
It's quiet up here.

02:32:12.090 --> 02:32:14.340
All you can hear is the wind

02:32:14.340 --> 02:32:17.953
and the sand that it whips
off these endless dunes.

02:32:17.953 --> 02:32:20.536
(sand rustles)

02:32:21.660 --> 02:32:24.843
But not far from where I
sleep there's a new sound.

02:32:24.843 --> 02:32:29.010
(construction rumbles and drones)

02:32:35.940 --> 02:32:37.020
For years I've been hearing

02:32:37.020 --> 02:32:39.750
about this trillion-dollar futuristic city

02:32:39.750 --> 02:32:43.443
that the kingdom of Saudi Arabia
is building atop this sand.

02:32:46.920 --> 02:32:49.110
They're calling it Neom,

02:32:49.110 --> 02:32:50.550
and they're investing a huge amount

02:32:50.550 --> 02:32:53.853
of their vast oil wealth
into making it come to life.

02:32:56.970 --> 02:33:00.030
I've been hearing about all
the reasons it can't work,

02:33:00.030 --> 02:33:03.513
how this is all just a
mirage of royal ambitions.

02:33:04.770 --> 02:33:06.810
And yet even from space,

02:33:06.810 --> 02:33:09.630
you can see that they're building it,

02:33:09.630 --> 02:33:10.980
they're building something.

02:33:17.130 --> 02:33:20.650
Why would anyone try to
build a futuristic city

02:33:21.510 --> 02:33:23.553
in a barren desert?

02:33:24.540 --> 02:33:26.970
That is a question I've
had for a very long time,

02:33:26.970 --> 02:33:29.464
and one I intend to get to the bottom of.

02:33:29.464 --> 02:33:31.140
(inquisitive music)

02:33:31.140 --> 02:33:34.020
So I'm going into Neom to
see what it looks like,

02:33:34.020 --> 02:33:36.373
to get a sense of its scale.

02:33:36.373 --> 02:33:40.230
We're looking at this
fleet of dump trucks.

02:33:40.230 --> 02:33:44.130
The amount of construction
activity is insane,

02:33:44.130 --> 02:33:47.220
and it hasn't stopped as we've
been driving along The Line.

02:33:47.220 --> 02:33:50.700
The magnitude, I've never
seen anything close.

02:33:50.700 --> 02:33:52.563
They're building mountains out there.

02:33:55.080 --> 02:33:56.610
I'm joined by my friend Solom,

02:33:56.610 --> 02:33:58.533
who was born in a tent out here.

02:34:00.450 --> 02:34:03.240
He knows this desert better than anyone,

02:34:03.240 --> 02:34:05.643
every dune, every valley.

02:34:07.680 --> 02:34:09.210
All the secret water spots

02:34:09.210 --> 02:34:11.823
hidden deep in red rock slot canyons.

02:34:15.510 --> 02:34:18.540
I asked Solom to bring
me out into the desert,

02:34:18.540 --> 02:34:21.570
not only to get up close on
the construction of Neom,

02:34:23.340 --> 02:34:25.920
but also to show me how
people live out here,

02:34:25.920 --> 02:34:29.040
to introduce me to members of his tribe,

02:34:29.040 --> 02:34:31.680
how they've survived for centuries.

02:34:31.680 --> 02:34:34.950
Nomadic Bedouin, who
represent the origin story

02:34:34.950 --> 02:34:36.481
of this desert kingdom.

02:34:36.481 --> 02:34:38.929
(Bedouin people laughing)

02:34:38.929 --> 02:34:40.320
I wanted to put a human face

02:34:40.320 --> 02:34:44.011
to this collision of worlds, old and new.

02:34:44.011 --> 02:34:46.761
(wondrous music)

02:34:50.970 --> 02:34:53.010
I believe this little shape on the map

02:34:53.010 --> 02:34:56.190
represents the story of Saudi Arabia,

02:34:56.190 --> 02:34:59.700
where it came from, how it got here,

02:34:59.700 --> 02:35:03.393
and why it is attempting to
build these cities in the sand.

02:35:14.208 --> 02:35:17.070
(truck horn blaring)

02:35:17.070 --> 02:35:18.720
I'm officially in Neom,

02:35:18.720 --> 02:35:21.267
and let me tell you
what I'm seeing so far.

02:35:21.267 --> 02:35:23.010
(trucks droning)

02:35:23.010 --> 02:35:25.710
Trucks, trucks, trucks.

02:35:25.710 --> 02:35:28.380
Dump trucks, cargo trucks,

02:35:28.380 --> 02:35:30.030
trucks carrying excavators,

02:35:30.030 --> 02:35:32.130
trucks carrying more trucks.

02:35:32.130 --> 02:35:35.100
I've never seen so many
trucks in my entire life.

02:35:35.100 --> 02:35:37.950
Oh, and these roads, a
lot of them are brand new,

02:35:37.950 --> 02:35:39.960
a lot of them don't even
show up on Google Maps.

02:35:39.960 --> 02:35:41.010
Like I'm looking at my phone

02:35:41.010 --> 02:35:43.440
and I'm just sort of
floating in no man's land

02:35:43.440 --> 02:35:45.450
because these are all brand-new roads.

02:35:45.450 --> 02:35:49.334
This is new, everything
is popping up so quickly.

02:35:49.334 --> 02:35:51.113
(laughs) There's so many trucks.

02:35:51.113 --> 02:35:53.696
(upbeat music)

02:35:54.540 --> 02:35:55.470
All of the sudden,

02:35:55.470 --> 02:35:59.430
Solom turned off one of
these pristinely new roads.

02:35:59.430 --> 02:36:01.260
Why do you deflate the tires?

02:36:01.260 --> 02:36:02.340
- For the dune.

02:36:02.340 --> 02:36:03.420
- We're now gonna start driving

02:36:03.420 --> 02:36:06.003
just into the desert.

02:36:06.003 --> 02:36:08.970
(bright music)

02:36:08.970 --> 02:36:10.623
Wow, this car can go everywhere.

02:36:17.250 --> 02:36:19.078
Are we going up this dune?
- Yeah.

02:36:19.078 --> 02:36:19.995
- Oh, wow.

02:36:21.618 --> 02:36:25.035
(engine revs)
- Oh, yeah.

02:36:26.810 --> 02:36:28.260
- Whoo. Geez, this is insane.

02:36:31.338 --> 02:36:34.171
(ethereal music)

02:36:39.967 --> 02:36:44.967
(Johnny laughs)
(bright music)

02:36:45.404 --> 02:36:46.237
Man.

02:36:47.223 --> 02:36:49.080
It's gorgeous.

02:36:49.080 --> 02:36:51.063
Okay, now we're really in the desert.

02:37:04.440 --> 02:37:06.810
We found a little canyon to camp in.

02:37:06.810 --> 02:37:09.900
(fire crackling)

02:37:09.900 --> 02:37:12.120
And while I thought we'd
be eating granola bars

02:37:12.120 --> 02:37:15.330
or canned soup, Solom had other plans.

02:37:15.330 --> 02:37:16.887
- [Solom] Have you
tried camel meat before?

02:37:16.887 --> 02:37:17.919
- [Johnny] Nuh-uh.

02:37:17.919 --> 02:37:19.285
- You will try it tonight.
- Really?

02:37:19.285 --> 02:37:21.869
- Hashi. Hashi is the young camel.

02:37:21.869 --> 02:37:24.810
- [Johnny] Ah, rice with camel meat.

02:37:24.810 --> 02:37:27.060
The evening tradition here is to wind down

02:37:27.060 --> 02:37:29.373
with a cup of coffee.

02:37:31.290 --> 02:37:35.223
Saudi coffee is unroasted and
green, infused with cardamom.

02:37:36.600 --> 02:37:38.400
It's a different taste, I like it though.

02:37:38.400 --> 02:37:40.563
But it's still very caffeinated.

02:37:41.910 --> 02:37:43.980
Oh, and then after the coffee comes...

02:37:43.980 --> 02:37:45.030
- Tea?
- Ah, thank you.

02:37:45.030 --> 02:37:47.190
Which, again, is very delicious,

02:37:47.190 --> 02:37:50.640
but how can they drink so
much coffee before bed?

02:37:50.640 --> 02:37:51.840
Yeah, I can't drink too much

02:37:51.840 --> 02:37:52.680
because I-
- Oh yeah.

02:37:52.680 --> 02:37:54.150
- The caffeine, I won't be able to sleep.

02:37:54.150 --> 02:37:54.983
- Yeah.

02:37:54.983 --> 02:37:56.280
- [Johnny] In the morning
I'll drink a lot of it.

02:37:56.280 --> 02:37:57.113
- Okay.

02:37:58.214 --> 02:38:00.797
(gentle music)

02:38:01.689 --> 02:38:05.648
- Wow.

02:38:05.648 --> 02:38:09.060
Mm, wow. That's good.

02:38:09.060 --> 02:38:12.480
So until what age did
you live in the desert?

02:38:12.480 --> 02:38:13.890
- Maybe until seven.

02:38:13.890 --> 02:38:15.418
- Till seven?
- Yeah.

02:38:15.418 --> 02:38:16.816
- Did you like it?
- Yeah, I like it.

02:38:16.816 --> 02:38:19.178
- Yeah.
- I like it.

02:38:19.178 --> 02:38:21.852
It's so rare to find families.

02:38:21.852 --> 02:38:24.062
Real families living in the desert.

02:38:24.062 --> 02:38:25.537
(Johnny laughing)

02:38:25.537 --> 02:38:28.852
The people that we are going
to, they are real Bedouins.

02:38:28.852 --> 02:38:31.272
(Bedouins people laughing)

02:38:31.272 --> 02:38:33.689
But when we moved to the city

02:38:35.340 --> 02:38:38.203
I study.
(cars honking)

02:38:39.090 --> 02:38:39.923
I go back.

02:38:39.923 --> 02:38:40.756
- Oh, you go back to the desert?

02:38:40.756 --> 02:38:41.589
- Yeah.

02:38:41.589 --> 02:38:43.950
(gentle music)

02:38:43.950 --> 02:38:47.190
Even for me, I like to
have a job in the city,

02:38:47.190 --> 02:38:51.060
but I want the Bedouin life.

02:38:51.060 --> 02:38:52.860
I want them to stay.

02:38:52.860 --> 02:38:54.630
- Soon we'll meet the Bedouin nomads

02:38:54.630 --> 02:38:57.060
who sleep under these stars every night.

02:38:57.060 --> 02:39:01.713
Look at this night full
of incredible stars.

02:39:03.907 --> 02:39:08.907
Wow.

02:39:11.700 --> 02:39:14.883
The Saudi desert is proving
to be exceptionally peaceful,

02:39:15.930 --> 02:39:18.750
despite the army of
trucks that, by the way,

02:39:18.750 --> 02:39:20.950
are still working here
in the dead of night.

02:39:23.430 --> 02:39:26.583
They don't ever stop
and haven't for years.

02:39:31.089 --> 02:39:33.839
(Johnny exhales)

02:39:37.463 --> 02:39:39.750
It is so silent here.

02:39:39.750 --> 02:39:41.820
My brain doesn't know what to do about it.

02:39:41.820 --> 02:39:43.490
It's so silent and...

02:39:44.514 --> 02:39:46.475
(serene music)

02:39:46.475 --> 02:39:47.820
(Johnny chuckles)

02:39:47.820 --> 02:39:49.200
But as the sun rises

02:39:49.200 --> 02:39:52.830
and Solom feeds me dates and green coffee,

02:39:52.830 --> 02:39:54.333
oh, and of course more tea,

02:39:55.230 --> 02:39:57.177
I'm thinking about my mission for today.

02:39:57.177 --> 02:39:58.830
(driving music)

02:39:58.830 --> 02:40:01.980
My plan is to drive all throughout Neom,

02:40:01.980 --> 02:40:03.870
to all the big construction sites

02:40:03.870 --> 02:40:06.420
and show you what they're
planning on building out here

02:40:06.420 --> 02:40:10.080
with their initial budget of $500 billion,

02:40:10.080 --> 02:40:14.013
which has since ballooned to
an estimated $1.5 trillion.

02:40:15.420 --> 02:40:17.430
And you're about to see why.

02:40:17.430 --> 02:40:18.600
So I made it to the coast,

02:40:18.600 --> 02:40:21.753
and I'm looking at the beautiful Red Sea,

02:40:22.590 --> 02:40:26.340
which is a very beautiful
kind of blue in fact.

02:40:26.340 --> 02:40:28.613
- [Presenter] This is Oxagon,

02:40:28.613 --> 02:40:32.087
the place where ideas
can change the world.

02:40:32.087 --> 02:40:33.480
- [Johnny] Oxagon is planned

02:40:33.480 --> 02:40:35.700
to be their futuristic industrial city.

02:40:35.700 --> 02:40:37.620
Half of it built on the water.

02:40:37.620 --> 02:40:40.290
It'll be a port where
shipping can connect the world

02:40:40.290 --> 02:40:42.510
to a bunch of high-tech manufacturing,

02:40:42.510 --> 02:40:44.283
research, and logistics.

02:40:45.990 --> 02:40:47.433
And there's Sindalah,

02:40:48.870 --> 02:40:51.180
which is gonna be right
here on the Red Sea.

02:40:51.180 --> 02:40:53.460
It's going to be a luxury resort island

02:40:53.460 --> 02:40:56.220
with plenty of parking for yachts.

02:40:56.220 --> 02:40:57.210
Hello.

02:40:57.210 --> 02:40:58.260
We picked up a hitchhiker

02:40:58.260 --> 02:41:00.660
who has been working
on Sindalah for years.

02:41:00.660 --> 02:41:02.373
How's it looking in Sindalah?

02:41:03.510 --> 02:41:05.340
- [Hitch Hiker] Finished like 99%.

02:41:05.340 --> 02:41:06.330
- Really?
- Yeah.

02:41:06.330 --> 02:41:09.235
(upbeat music)

02:41:09.235 --> 02:41:10.068
Sindalah.

02:41:11.974 --> 02:41:14.557
(drone buzzes)

02:41:17.550 --> 02:41:18.896
And then there's Magna,

02:41:18.896 --> 02:41:20.100
(light fluttery music)

02:41:20.100 --> 02:41:22.920
which is actually the name of
one of these coastal villages

02:41:22.920 --> 02:41:27.330
we've just been driving next
to that is no longer a village.

02:41:27.330 --> 02:41:29.670
They forcibly relocated
thousands of people

02:41:29.670 --> 02:41:30.520
for this project.

02:41:33.660 --> 02:41:36.540
Atop the ruins of these
villages, the kingdom

02:41:36.540 --> 02:41:40.730
is building 12 luxury coastal
resorts and communities.

02:41:40.730 --> 02:41:43.890
(birds squawking)

02:41:43.890 --> 02:41:46.590
Hey, just one quick clarification here,

02:41:46.590 --> 02:41:49.500
all of those visuals are 3D renders.

02:41:49.500 --> 02:41:52.230
They are visualizations done by Neom

02:41:52.230 --> 02:41:54.720
to see what they want this to look like.

02:41:54.720 --> 02:41:56.190
This stuff doesn't exist yet,

02:41:56.190 --> 02:41:57.900
and I think that maybe is obvious,

02:41:57.900 --> 02:41:59.640
but it looks so real

02:41:59.640 --> 02:42:01.890
that I just want to
just make crystal clear.

02:42:01.890 --> 02:42:02.970
I'm showing a lot of this stuff

02:42:02.970 --> 02:42:06.030
from the hypey promo visuals

02:42:06.030 --> 02:42:07.380
that have come out from Neom.

02:42:07.380 --> 02:42:08.700
So that's one thing.

02:42:08.700 --> 02:42:11.220
Also, check out some of this footage I got

02:42:11.220 --> 02:42:12.780
while I was on a dune.

02:42:12.780 --> 02:42:14.580
It's floating around on this dune.

02:42:14.580 --> 02:42:16.470
This is me shooting from the drone,

02:42:16.470 --> 02:42:19.110
but suddenly on the other
side of this massive dune,

02:42:19.110 --> 02:42:21.633
there are these green circles.

02:42:22.680 --> 02:42:24.750
When I was planning my
trip to Saudi Arabia,

02:42:24.750 --> 02:42:26.400
I got really interested in these things,

02:42:26.400 --> 02:42:28.140
but that has nothing to do with Neom,

02:42:28.140 --> 02:42:31.440
so I hopped on the phone with
my new colleague, Christophe,

02:42:31.440 --> 02:42:33.660
and asked him to go down
the deep dive rabbit hole

02:42:33.660 --> 02:42:36.600
of why there's a bunch of green
circles in the Saudi desert.

02:42:36.600 --> 02:42:37.470
And he did so,

02:42:37.470 --> 02:42:39.870
and he made an entire video
about what he learned.

02:42:39.870 --> 02:42:41.250
That video is live right now

02:42:41.250 --> 02:42:42.660
on our new channel tunnel_vision,

02:42:42.660 --> 02:42:43.725
I think you're gonna like it.

02:42:43.725 --> 02:42:45.060
(bright music)

02:42:45.060 --> 02:42:50.060
Built on some pretty wild
designs, ideas and concepts,

02:42:51.420 --> 02:42:52.253
including...

02:42:52.253 --> 02:42:55.800
- [Presenter] A vast
subterranean digital community

02:42:55.800 --> 02:42:57.750
inside a mountain,

02:42:57.750 --> 02:42:59.817
Magna, the coast of Neom.

02:43:04.530 --> 02:43:06.365
- [Johnny] Yeah, right there is perfect.

02:43:06.365 --> 02:43:10.532
(Solom speaking foreign language)

02:43:11.790 --> 02:43:15.270
- And then there's Trojena
up here in the mountains.

02:43:15.270 --> 02:43:17.910
Yes, there are big mountains here.

02:43:17.910 --> 02:43:20.400
It's going to be a luxury
tourist destination

02:43:20.400 --> 02:43:22.830
for adventure and wellness retreats,

02:43:22.830 --> 02:43:26.130
complete with an artificial
lake and outdoor activities

02:43:26.130 --> 02:43:28.320
like mountain biking and skiing.

02:43:28.320 --> 02:43:30.750
Yes, I just said skiing.

02:43:30.750 --> 02:43:33.420
Up here in the mountains it
gets kind of cold in the winter

02:43:33.420 --> 02:43:35.490
and snow falls.

02:43:35.490 --> 02:43:37.290
Definitely not enough for skiing,

02:43:37.290 --> 02:43:40.983
but enough for them to make
fake snow and make ski slopes.

02:43:42.000 --> 02:43:43.620
They're building a manmade lake,

02:43:43.620 --> 02:43:44.790
in fact, they're doing it right now,

02:43:44.790 --> 02:43:46.020
I'm looking up at the mountain

02:43:46.020 --> 02:43:48.060
and seeing the plumes of
smoke from the dynamite

02:43:48.060 --> 02:43:48.893
that they're using

02:43:48.893 --> 02:43:50.640
(explosions boom)

02:43:50.640 --> 02:43:54.330
to do whatever they need to do
to build an artificial lake.

02:43:54.330 --> 02:43:55.770
Oh, and by the way,

02:43:55.770 --> 02:43:57.570
that's the mountain
where Moses talked to God

02:43:57.570 --> 02:44:00.120
and got the 10 Commandments
like a few thousand years ago.

02:44:00.120 --> 02:44:03.173
- There is no freedom without the law.

02:44:03.173 --> 02:44:05.673
(lofty music)

02:44:07.366 --> 02:44:10.050
Who is on the Lord's side?

02:44:10.050 --> 02:44:13.688
- [Presenter] Welcome to
Trojena, the mountains of Neom.

02:44:13.688 --> 02:44:16.170
(Johnny laughing)

02:44:16.170 --> 02:44:17.613
- This is all so strange.

02:44:23.413 --> 02:44:26.246
(Johnny laughing)

02:44:31.020 --> 02:44:33.351
And then of course, the one
you've maybe heard about.

02:44:33.351 --> 02:44:36.268
(grand airy music)

02:44:43.890 --> 02:44:45.933
And yes, it's being built.

02:44:52.890 --> 02:44:55.500
I've been driving along it all morning

02:44:55.500 --> 02:44:58.383
and I can't really fathom what I'm seeing.

02:45:00.540 --> 02:45:04.383
I continue to have my mind
blown at a scale of this.

02:45:08.370 --> 02:45:10.830
They say The Line is
gonna be a futuristic city

02:45:10.830 --> 02:45:14.070
that stretches 170 kilometers

02:45:14.070 --> 02:45:18.540
and run on renewable energy
and artificial intelligence.

02:45:18.540 --> 02:45:20.940
They want 10 million
people to come live here,

02:45:20.940 --> 02:45:22.770
like a whole New York city of people

02:45:22.770 --> 02:45:25.533
inside of this line in the desert.

02:45:33.570 --> 02:45:34.890
Just the digging alone,

02:45:34.890 --> 02:45:38.313
the amount of earth that
they're moving is jaw dropping.

02:45:44.280 --> 02:45:48.540
The magnitude is like they're
building mountains out there

02:45:48.540 --> 02:45:50.793
and moving so much earth.

02:45:51.878 --> 02:45:54.628
(crashing music)

02:45:55.620 --> 02:45:57.840
Now I thought I had seen
a lot of trucks before,

02:45:57.840 --> 02:46:00.030
but as I drove along The Line,

02:46:00.030 --> 02:46:01.893
I started to get slightly dizzy.

02:46:02.730 --> 02:46:06.870
Having a hard time processing
the scale of all of this,

02:46:06.870 --> 02:46:10.140
all of these machines working nonstop,

02:46:10.140 --> 02:46:13.830
every hour of every day for years.

02:46:13.830 --> 02:46:16.450
Moving sand, making mountains

02:46:17.790 --> 02:46:20.703
and carving a straight
line into the desert.

02:46:23.590 --> 02:46:26.257
(wind rustling)

02:46:27.270 --> 02:46:31.020
All of this seems impossible.

02:46:31.020 --> 02:46:33.000
So much of the reporting on Neom

02:46:33.000 --> 02:46:36.750
has been focused on how
implausible, expensive,

02:46:36.750 --> 02:46:39.510
and delusional this project is.

02:46:39.510 --> 02:46:41.460
And that's all well and good

02:46:41.460 --> 02:46:42.750
if that's what you wanna focus on,

02:46:42.750 --> 02:46:44.910
but to me that's not that interesting.

02:46:44.910 --> 02:46:48.868
What I'm most interested
in understanding is why.

02:46:48.868 --> 02:46:51.535
(gentle music)

02:46:56.430 --> 02:46:59.343
We found a little cave
last night and camped here.

02:47:02.160 --> 02:47:03.780
Solom invited some of his friends out.

02:47:03.780 --> 02:47:04.890
Hey guys.
- Hi.

02:47:04.890 --> 02:47:06.180
- How's it going?
- Good.

02:47:06.180 --> 02:47:08.014
- This is so cool, right?
- Yeah, it is.

02:47:08.014 --> 02:47:09.300
(fire crackling)

02:47:09.300 --> 02:47:11.310
- [Johnny] We all sat around the fire,

02:47:11.310 --> 02:47:14.835
me listening while they
laughed and joked in Arabic.

02:47:14.835 --> 02:47:19.168
(Solom and friends speaking Arabic)

02:47:21.570 --> 02:47:24.333
And then began cooking yet
another delicious meal.

02:47:25.200 --> 02:47:30.030
This time, goat cooked in
goat milk with flatbread.

02:47:30.030 --> 02:47:32.369
Wow, this is awesome.
- Yeah.

02:47:32.369 --> 02:47:35.702
(Solom speaking Arabic)

02:47:38.255 --> 02:47:39.838
- Mm, wow. So good.

02:47:45.300 --> 02:47:46.800
I'm admittedly a little groggy

02:47:46.800 --> 02:47:49.343
after sleeping a few
nights on the desert floor.

02:47:49.343 --> 02:47:51.760
(warm music)

02:47:54.030 --> 02:47:58.260
We're just sledding down
a massive dune in a car

02:47:58.260 --> 02:48:00.333
and it's as smooth as butter.

02:48:05.490 --> 02:48:08.610
Where they're making Trojena
it's gonna be kind of crazy.

02:48:08.610 --> 02:48:11.763
To have skiing in Saudi Arabia, right?

02:48:15.657 --> 02:48:18.593
- That's a big change here.
- Yeah.

02:48:18.593 --> 02:48:21.333
- It was like a hiking trail.
- Yeah.

02:48:22.440 --> 02:48:24.077
Really?

02:48:24.077 --> 02:48:27.494
- [Solom] But with this, no, this is new.

02:48:30.087 --> 02:48:32.070
- Today, Solom is introducing me

02:48:32.070 --> 02:48:33.783
to members of his tribe.

02:48:35.010 --> 02:48:36.615
Is this them?

02:48:36.615 --> 02:48:37.448
Hello.

02:48:37.448 --> 02:48:38.820
It'll be the polar opposite

02:48:38.820 --> 02:48:41.703
of yesterday's tour through
Neom's construction.

02:48:42.990 --> 02:48:44.970
He put on his traditional Saudi clothing

02:48:44.970 --> 02:48:47.190
so as not to surprise
the members of his tribe

02:48:47.190 --> 02:48:50.193
that he has indeed
become a modern city man.

02:48:52.032 --> 02:48:54.240
(gentle music)

02:48:54.240 --> 02:48:56.220
We arrived to a remote patch of desert

02:48:56.220 --> 02:48:57.813
where a few tents are set up.

02:49:01.020 --> 02:49:04.050
The elder of this family
warmly invites me in.

02:49:04.050 --> 02:49:05.520
Johnny, hi.

02:49:05.520 --> 02:49:06.843
To sit for coffee.

02:49:09.767 --> 02:49:11.520
(Bedouin peoples speaking Arabic)

02:49:11.520 --> 02:49:14.307
And then of course more tea.

02:49:14.307 --> 02:49:15.360
Wow.

02:49:15.360 --> 02:49:17.973
And at this point, I'm just
embracing the caffeine.

02:49:19.530 --> 02:49:21.060
They tell me that the only Westerners

02:49:21.060 --> 02:49:23.907
they've ever seen out here
are the ones looking for oil.

02:49:23.907 --> 02:49:26.550
And that it's very strange
to see me showing up

02:49:26.550 --> 02:49:30.076
to learn about their culture
and especially with a camera.

02:49:30.076 --> 02:49:34.243
(Bedouin peoples speaking Arabic)

02:49:39.240 --> 02:49:41.390
- [Johnny] So how long
have you lived here?

02:49:42.412 --> 02:49:43.245
- [Tribesman] This is two months here.

02:49:43.245 --> 02:49:44.100
- Months?
- Yeah.

02:49:44.100 --> 02:49:48.387
- Wow.
(Tribesman speaking Arabic)

02:49:48.387 --> 02:49:50.145
(sheep bleating)

02:49:50.145 --> 02:49:51.900
- I got to meet their animals,

02:49:51.900 --> 02:49:54.383
extended members of this nomadic family.

02:49:54.383 --> 02:49:57.540
(camel grunting)

02:49:57.540 --> 02:49:59.820
So this family's been here on this site,

02:49:59.820 --> 02:50:04.533
and they'll move again when
the season tells them to.

02:50:05.859 --> 02:50:09.328
They basically are here
grazing their camels

02:50:09.328 --> 02:50:11.460
and their goats.

02:50:11.460 --> 02:50:12.650
And God, they've got a beautiful view.

02:50:12.650 --> 02:50:14.493
Oh, my God, those mountains.

02:50:17.400 --> 02:50:19.350
So wait, he's the shepherd?

02:50:19.350 --> 02:50:20.550
You're the shepherd?

02:50:20.550 --> 02:50:23.160
- Yeah.
- Wow. That's cool.

02:50:23.160 --> 02:50:25.233
Do you ever get lost in the desert?

02:50:25.233 --> 02:50:27.477
(Solom speaking Arabic)

02:50:27.477 --> 02:50:29.730
- No.
- No, he's a pro.

02:50:29.730 --> 02:50:31.203
He's a pro on that donkey.

02:50:33.348 --> 02:50:34.710
(Bedouin people speaking Arabic)

02:50:34.710 --> 02:50:38.040
- These people's lives depend
on moving these animals

02:50:38.040 --> 02:50:40.953
through the desert in
search for scarce food.

02:50:43.170 --> 02:50:45.510
A theme I hear from a lot
of people who live out here

02:50:45.510 --> 02:50:50.510
is the, just sense of peace
and simplicity that they get,

02:50:52.710 --> 02:50:56.130
that they realize that the
city could never bring.

02:50:56.130 --> 02:50:59.306
What do you like about out
here that makes you stay?

02:50:59.306 --> 02:51:02.973
(tribesman speaking Arabic)

02:51:06.330 --> 02:51:08.280
So when you go to the city-

02:51:08.280 --> 02:51:09.940
- Ah, city.
- City, no.

02:51:09.940 --> 02:51:11.900
(both laugh)

02:51:11.900 --> 02:51:13.000
This is a better life.

02:51:14.250 --> 02:51:17.873
Man, this sunset is gorgeous.

02:51:17.873 --> 02:51:20.873
(tribesman singing)

02:51:23.597 --> 02:51:26.097
Okay. Yeah, yeah, that's good.

02:51:32.171 --> 02:51:35.588
(warm adventurous music)

02:51:37.380 --> 02:51:39.240
As the group faces east

02:51:39.240 --> 02:51:43.710
to perform their fourth of
five prayers they do each day,

02:51:43.710 --> 02:51:44.829
I'm looking west.

02:51:44.829 --> 02:51:47.760
(gentle music)

02:51:47.760 --> 02:51:49.230
Just over those mountains,

02:51:49.230 --> 02:51:50.580
the kingdom of Saudi Arabia

02:51:50.580 --> 02:51:53.103
is digging a giant line in the sand.

02:52:00.180 --> 02:52:03.420
Working day and night
to reshape this desert,

02:52:03.420 --> 02:52:05.583
building their high-tech eco city.

02:52:16.440 --> 02:52:18.003
For most of its history,

02:52:18.870 --> 02:52:21.783
this was the way of life
out here on this peninsula.

02:52:23.400 --> 02:52:26.490
And it may have remained such to this day,

02:52:26.490 --> 02:52:29.820
but eventually the Bedouin
on this peninsula united,

02:52:29.820 --> 02:52:31.860
and unbeknownst to anyone,

02:52:31.860 --> 02:52:34.503
was sitting atop a sea of black gold.

02:52:35.700 --> 02:52:37.020
- [Narrator] Oil in commercial quantities,

02:52:37.020 --> 02:52:39.753
averaging about 200,000 barrels a day.

02:52:40.980 --> 02:52:42.450
To handle this huge volume,

02:52:42.450 --> 02:52:44.910
the expanding program requires an increase

02:52:44.910 --> 02:52:46.110
of permanent personnel

02:52:46.110 --> 02:52:48.867
to a total of more than 15,000 Arabs.

02:52:51.090 --> 02:52:52.803
- [Johnny] Oil changed everything.

02:52:54.120 --> 02:52:56.130
The kingdom rapidly modernized.

02:52:56.130 --> 02:52:58.440
- [Narrator] The king
sees the oil development

02:52:58.440 --> 02:53:01.410
as the greatest single means
to modernize his country

02:53:01.410 --> 02:53:03.783
and improve the living
standards of his people.

02:53:04.620 --> 02:53:07.050
- [Johnny] All while still
maintaining a deep connection

02:53:07.050 --> 02:53:08.719
with Islamic law,

02:53:08.719 --> 02:53:10.383
(muezzin sings)

02:53:10.383 --> 02:53:13.623
and the rules and norms of Bedouin life.

02:53:15.480 --> 02:53:17.580
And despite this rapid modernization,

02:53:17.580 --> 02:53:19.230
hundreds of thousands of people

02:53:19.230 --> 02:53:21.753
still maintain the nomadic lifestyle.

02:53:23.250 --> 02:53:25.143
It is a link to their past.

02:53:29.218 --> 02:53:33.051
(tribesman chatter in Arabic)

02:53:34.980 --> 02:53:39.980
Nice.

02:53:45.318 --> 02:53:47.465
(tribesman chatter in Arabic)

02:53:47.465 --> 02:53:48.780
- [Solom] They're talking about something

02:53:48.780 --> 02:53:51.227
that they see it these days.

02:53:51.227 --> 02:53:53.160
The people travel by like bike-

02:53:53.160 --> 02:53:54.910
- Cycling? Oh.
- Yeah.

02:53:56.258 --> 02:53:58.592
- The conversation goes into the night

02:53:58.592 --> 02:54:00.450
and I knew I had to ask them about Neom.

02:54:00.450 --> 02:54:02.060
Can you ask them what their thoughts are

02:54:02.060 --> 02:54:04.620
on all the building that's happening?

02:54:04.620 --> 02:54:06.870
I have to be sensitive here

02:54:06.870 --> 02:54:08.433
'cause I'm reminded where I am.

02:54:09.390 --> 02:54:13.470
Saudi Arabia isn't a place
where citizens, nomads or not,

02:54:13.470 --> 02:54:15.873
can speak critically about their kingdom.

02:54:17.820 --> 02:54:20.673
I got nervous looks and vague answers.

02:54:21.510 --> 02:54:23.460
But one thing is for certain

02:54:23.460 --> 02:54:24.600
that the Saudi government

02:54:24.600 --> 02:54:27.420
has been paying and compensating anyone

02:54:27.420 --> 02:54:30.541
who has been affected by these projects.

02:54:30.541 --> 02:54:34.208
(tribesmen speaking Arabic)

02:54:38.280 --> 02:54:40.860
- [Johnny] Out of respect,
I decide not to push it,

02:54:40.860 --> 02:54:41.976
it's not worth it.

02:54:41.976 --> 02:54:45.643
(tribesmen speaking Arabic)

02:54:50.310 --> 02:54:51.840
- [Johnny] We're sitting
around a huge platter

02:54:51.840 --> 02:54:53.850
of camel meat and fragrant rice.

02:54:53.850 --> 02:54:55.849
So where I'm from, there's no camels.

02:54:55.849 --> 02:54:57.600
- Ah.
- No camels,

02:54:57.600 --> 02:55:00.510
I've never seen a camel until yesterday.

02:55:00.510 --> 02:55:03.600
The elder generously
showing his hospitality

02:55:03.600 --> 02:55:06.630
by finding the juiciest
chunks of camel meat and fat

02:55:06.630 --> 02:55:07.920
and passing them to me.

02:55:07.920 --> 02:55:08.753
Thank you.

02:55:10.039 --> 02:55:11.577
(elder speaking Arabic)
- Yeah.

02:55:11.577 --> 02:55:14.190
And I'm realizing that
this moment is a glimpse

02:55:14.190 --> 02:55:16.933
into one of the oldest-surviving
cultures on earth.

02:55:16.933 --> 02:55:20.266
(elder speaking Arabic)

02:55:24.090 --> 02:55:25.054
- Thank you.

02:55:25.054 --> 02:55:28.451
(Bedouin people speaking Arabic)

02:55:28.451 --> 02:55:29.970
(gentle music)

02:55:29.970 --> 02:55:32.760
- The family invites us
to sleep near their tent.

02:55:32.760 --> 02:55:36.093
Another night, another sky full of stars.

02:55:38.130 --> 02:55:40.170
I'm really feeling it,

02:55:40.170 --> 02:55:44.100
just how fast the change
has come to this desert,

02:55:44.100 --> 02:55:45.930
where nomads whose movement

02:55:45.930 --> 02:55:49.810
and lifestyle hark back to
the earliest civilizations

02:55:51.120 --> 02:55:53.740
and who are now living
a stone's throw away

02:55:55.140 --> 02:55:57.940
from the construction of a futuristic city

02:55:59.010 --> 02:56:01.890
that is attempting to
define the next chapter

02:56:01.890 --> 02:56:03.410
of human civilization.

02:56:03.410 --> 02:56:06.410
(distressing music)

02:56:11.481 --> 02:56:13.397
It feels like too much to process.

02:56:13.397 --> 02:56:16.064
(gentle music)

02:56:22.507 --> 02:56:23.790
(elder speaking Arabic)

02:56:23.790 --> 02:56:25.500
- I couldn't help but feel worried

02:56:25.500 --> 02:56:27.923
for this link to the past.

02:56:27.923 --> 02:56:31.562
(Bedouin people speaking Arabic)

02:56:31.562 --> 02:56:35.979
(Johnny and Bedouin people laughing)

02:56:38.773 --> 02:56:41.606
(wind whispering)

02:56:44.317 --> 02:56:46.620
- So oil transformed this desert,

02:56:46.620 --> 02:56:49.428
but soon a problem began to arise.

02:56:49.428 --> 02:56:52.095
(serious music)

02:57:04.140 --> 02:57:05.640
Slowly, the world has started

02:57:05.640 --> 02:57:07.260
coming to terms with the reality

02:57:07.260 --> 02:57:11.250
that burning oil to run our world

02:57:11.250 --> 02:57:12.270
is turning the planet

02:57:12.270 --> 02:57:15.393
into a place that's not
conducive for human life.

02:57:19.023 --> 02:57:20.040
(futuristic music)

02:57:20.040 --> 02:57:22.050
The International Energy Agency predicts

02:57:22.050 --> 02:57:25.710
that the world will hit
peak oil demand in 2030,

02:57:25.710 --> 02:57:28.323
at which time demand will start to fall.

02:57:32.970 --> 02:57:34.590
Other predictions say it'll take longer,

02:57:34.590 --> 02:57:36.090
but everyone agrees

02:57:36.090 --> 02:57:39.273
that the future of this world is not oil.

02:57:40.530 --> 02:57:42.960
This is bad news for Saudi Arabia,

02:57:42.960 --> 02:57:45.630
a country that has nothing but oil.

02:57:45.630 --> 02:57:48.630
- [Announcer] Aramco, the
state-owned oil company,

02:57:48.630 --> 02:57:51.690
second-quarter profit are
tumbling by more than a third

02:57:51.690 --> 02:57:53.400
from last year's record highs.

02:57:53.400 --> 02:57:56.070
- Oil transformed this desert kingdom

02:57:56.070 --> 02:57:59.070
into a modern, powerful country.

02:57:59.070 --> 02:58:01.380
And in order for them
to retain that status,

02:58:01.380 --> 02:58:04.200
they're going to need to
use the money from that oil

02:58:04.200 --> 02:58:06.349
to transform themselves again.

02:58:06.349 --> 02:58:07.349
Enter, Neom.

02:58:08.479 --> 02:58:11.396
(futuristic music)

02:58:14.820 --> 02:58:18.150
Saudi Arabia wants to
replace its oil economy

02:58:18.150 --> 02:58:21.270
with new economies, new industries,

02:58:21.270 --> 02:58:23.733
some of which they want to invent.

02:58:26.280 --> 02:58:30.300
This kingdom that has been
historically closed is now open,

02:58:30.300 --> 02:58:32.400
welcoming tourists for the first time.

02:58:32.400 --> 02:58:35.850
- [Presenter] Welcome to
Arabia. Get your visa now.

02:58:35.850 --> 02:58:37.690
- Let's go to Sindalah!

02:58:37.690 --> 02:58:38.523
Are here.

02:58:40.080 --> 02:58:42.510
- [Johnny] Hoping to attract the world,

02:58:42.510 --> 02:58:45.870
this is something neighboring
Dubai has done successfully.

02:58:45.870 --> 02:58:47.570
- [Presenter] The spirit of Dubai.

02:58:49.050 --> 02:58:51.800
- But Saudi Arabia plans to
do it on a whole new level.

02:59:00.630 --> 02:59:01.830
I'll tell you one thing,

02:59:03.390 --> 02:59:05.283
the water here is beautiful.

02:59:07.400 --> 02:59:11.010
On that note, I was surprised
at how welcome I felt here.

02:59:11.010 --> 02:59:13.740
Every person I met
greeted me with kindness

02:59:13.740 --> 02:59:15.843
and eagerness to show me their culture.

02:59:18.845 --> 02:59:21.600
(tribesman chatter in Arabic)

02:59:21.600 --> 02:59:23.313
But it's not just about tourism,

02:59:24.900 --> 02:59:28.230
Neom is manufacturing,
sustainability technology,

02:59:28.230 --> 02:59:30.960
sports, education, media,

02:59:30.960 --> 02:59:34.004
and even an attempt to
reinvent cities themselves.

02:59:34.004 --> 02:59:37.087
(inquisitive music)

02:59:38.970 --> 02:59:40.560
They also want to use all this oil money

02:59:40.560 --> 02:59:43.020
to stay on top of global energy.

02:59:43.020 --> 02:59:46.833
All of this hot sun is
great for solar energy.

02:59:50.820 --> 02:59:51.840
And while driving around,

02:59:51.840 --> 02:59:54.663
I saw endless fields of wind turbines.

02:59:58.830 --> 03:00:01.650
And they can use this to make
new forms of hydrogen energy

03:00:01.650 --> 03:00:03.543
that they can ship around the world.

03:00:10.170 --> 03:00:12.120
And the reason why all these promo videos

03:00:12.120 --> 03:00:14.160
look like a Hollywood movie

03:00:14.160 --> 03:00:15.690
is because they can't do this

03:00:15.690 --> 03:00:18.056
without investors from the outside world.

03:00:18.056 --> 03:00:21.723
(presenter speaking Arabic)

03:00:24.544 --> 03:00:26.070
- [Johnny] They need
people to come live here.

03:00:26.070 --> 03:00:27.180
- [Vlogger] So my
husband got offered a job

03:00:27.180 --> 03:00:28.860
for Neom in Saudi Arabia,

03:00:28.860 --> 03:00:30.930
and we decided to make the move.

03:00:32.070 --> 03:00:34.380
- [Johnny] They need them to play here,

03:00:34.380 --> 03:00:37.173
raise children here,
somehow have a life here,

03:00:38.370 --> 03:00:41.460
and most crucially to
invest in these projects.

03:00:41.460 --> 03:00:42.330
- And what are we calling it?

03:00:42.330 --> 03:00:43.448
- The Line.

03:00:43.448 --> 03:00:45.090
- The Line.
- The Line.

03:00:45.090 --> 03:00:46.710
- [Johnny] So not unlike a startup,

03:00:46.710 --> 03:00:48.180
Neom is pitching the world

03:00:48.180 --> 03:00:50.310
on this impossible-sounding vision

03:00:50.310 --> 03:00:53.043
with the hopes that people
will buy it, invest in it,

03:00:55.290 --> 03:00:57.603
and make some version of it a reality.

03:01:04.620 --> 03:01:07.380
So do you feel excited about it Neom?

03:01:07.380 --> 03:01:08.213
Like-
- Yes.

03:01:08.213 --> 03:01:09.570
- Yeah? Why?

03:01:09.570 --> 03:01:12.630
- I wouldn't do this
business without Neom.

03:01:12.630 --> 03:01:13.590
- Oh wow.
- Before Neom.

03:01:13.590 --> 03:01:15.420
- Wow.
- Everyone wanna, like,

03:01:15.420 --> 03:01:17.400
check out on internet what is Neom.

03:01:17.400 --> 03:01:19.620
Then they need a tour guide.

03:01:19.620 --> 03:01:23.700
To have more than 30 tour
guides working with me.

03:01:23.700 --> 03:01:25.980
- Wow.
- So it's a big business now

03:01:25.980 --> 03:01:27.903
and it's all because of Neom.

03:01:30.900 --> 03:01:31.733
- [Johnny] So while Solom

03:01:31.733 --> 03:01:33.573
is seeing the benefits of this project,

03:01:34.470 --> 03:01:37.220
the effects for others in
this region are less certain.

03:01:39.750 --> 03:01:41.370
We're driving by some of the towns

03:01:41.370 --> 03:01:42.753
that have been demolished.

03:01:44.610 --> 03:01:45.900
The people forced to move

03:01:45.900 --> 03:01:48.520
to make way for the construction of Neom.

03:01:49.892 --> 03:01:52.892
They were given money to rebuild
their lives somewhere else.

03:01:54.270 --> 03:01:56.850
But some refused to leave,

03:01:56.850 --> 03:02:00.093
signing a petition to
protest this forced eviction.

03:02:01.830 --> 03:02:06.150
One even posted to social
media voicing his outrage,

03:02:06.150 --> 03:02:08.850
a risky move in a place
where freedom of speech

03:02:08.850 --> 03:02:10.325
isn't really a thing.

03:02:10.325 --> 03:02:11.310
(gunshots pop)

03:02:11.310 --> 03:02:13.170
Soon he was locked in a shootout

03:02:13.170 --> 03:02:15.513
with security forces that left him dead.

03:02:16.530 --> 03:02:18.090
- [Reporter] This is when Saudi officials

03:02:18.090 --> 03:02:19.740
reportedly handed over the body

03:02:19.740 --> 03:02:22.380
of Abdul Rahim al-Huwaiti to his tribe.

03:02:22.380 --> 03:02:24.780
His family denies the official narrative

03:02:24.780 --> 03:02:26.910
that Rahim was a terrorist.

03:02:26.910 --> 03:02:28.560
- [Johnny] At least 47 other villagers

03:02:28.560 --> 03:02:31.410
were detained on terror-related charges,

03:02:31.410 --> 03:02:33.585
and five of them are now on death row.

03:02:33.585 --> 03:02:37.085
(gentle optimistic music)

03:02:43.320 --> 03:02:46.080
The scale and riskiness of these projects

03:02:46.080 --> 03:02:50.250
speaks to the scale of
Saudi Arabia's challenge.

03:02:50.250 --> 03:02:53.490
They need something to keep
them afloat after oil goes away,

03:02:53.490 --> 03:02:55.560
so they're betting the family farm

03:02:55.560 --> 03:02:57.723
on a few massive projects.

03:02:59.130 --> 03:03:00.630
Many will likely fail,

03:03:00.630 --> 03:03:03.750
but even if a few of
these work out as planned,

03:03:03.750 --> 03:03:05.703
it could be their next oil.

03:03:10.110 --> 03:03:12.750
Lifting this kingdom into the future,

03:03:12.750 --> 03:03:14.163
that's the calculus here.

03:03:32.880 --> 03:03:36.390
But what's clear is that this kingdom

03:03:36.390 --> 03:03:38.313
isn't stopping anytime soon.

03:03:39.377 --> 03:03:42.294
(grand airy music)

03:04:07.054 --> 03:04:09.390
- Our economy is in recession

03:04:09.390 --> 03:04:12.693
and the civilized world
faces unprecedented dangers.

03:04:13.590 --> 03:04:17.595
Yet the state of our union
has never been stronger.

03:04:17.595 --> 03:04:22.595
(crowd cheering)
(subdued music)

03:04:44.993 --> 03:04:48.493
(subdued music continues)

03:05:14.995 --> 03:05:18.495
(subdued music continues)

03:05:45.013 --> 03:05:48.513
(subdued music continues)

